-- The Statecraft Times: first 50 stories (published) — rewritten in English from international reporting with attribution
-- Supabase SQL 에디터에서 실행 (seed-statecrafttimes.sql 다음). 여러 번 실행해도 된다 (같은 제목 기사는 건너뛴다)
--   · 출처: The Diplomat · Foreign Policy · Atlantic Council · U.S. State Department · Al Jazeera · BBC News · The Guardian 공개 기사.
--           번역·복사가 아니라 사실만 골라 새로 썼고 본문에 출처를 밝혔다. 원문 사진은 쓰지 않았다
--   · 사진: 위키미디어 공용의 공개 라이선스 사진(퍼블릭 도메인·CC0·CC BY·CC BY-SA), 사진 설명에 작가·라이선스 표기
--   · 상태 '발행'(바로 홈페이지에 나간다), 기자명 'Statecraft Times Staff', 편집 메모에 확인할 점·원문 주소
--   · 섹션: africa 2, americas 4, analysis 2, central-asia 1, china 1, defense 1, diplomacy 1, economy 1, europe 5, intelligence 4, japan 3, korea 2, middle-east 4, multilateral 2, nuclear 1, opinion 1, sanctions 1, south-asia 2, southeast-asia 3, technology 1, trade 2, ukraine-war 3, us-policy 3

with ctx as (
  select o.id as outlet_id,
         coalesce(
           (select p.id from profiles p where p.outlet_id = o.id and p.role = 'admin' order by p.created_at limit 1),
           (select p.id from profiles p where p.outlet_id = o.id and p.role = 'editor' order by p.created_at limit 1),
           (select p.id from profiles p where (to_jsonb(p) ->> 'is_super') = 'true' order by p.created_at limit 1),
           (select p.id from profiles p where p.role = 'admin' order by p.created_at limit 1)
         ) as author_id
  from outlets o
  where o.name = 'The Statecraft Times'
)
insert into articles (outlet_id, category_id, author_id, title, body, excerpt, thumbnail_url, status, published_at, tags)
select ctx.outlet_id, c.id, ctx.author_id, v.title, v.body, v.excerpt, v.thumb, 'published', least(v.published_at, now()), v.tags
from ctx
cross join (values
  ('us-policy', 'Trump Pairs Allied Investment Pledges With Midterm Campaign Events, Analysis Says', '<p>Trump has been unveiling Japanese and South Korean investment in the United States alongside Republican Senate candidates in the run-up to the midterm elections, according to an analysis published by The Diplomat. The piece argues that allied pledges negotiated over many months are being recast as immediate economic victories for the campaign season.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/2646/3824994628_f378ee4ddd_b.jpg" alt="The Trans-Alaska Pipeline near Fairbanks, Alaska."><p><em>The Trans-Alaska Pipeline near Fairbanks, Alaska. File photo. (Photo: roger4336 / Flickr, CC BY-SA 2.0)</em></p><p>On Sept. 28, Trump hosted Iowa Rep. Ashley Hinson, the Republican seeking an open Senate seat, in the Oval Office to announce a $15 billion steel plant in her state that the local subsidiary of an Indian conglomerate is building. Two days later, with Alaska Sen. Dan Sullivan beside him, the president announced about $54 billion in potential South Korean investment in the Alaska LNG pipeline project. The Associated Press noted it was “the second time in three days” Trump had linked a major investment with a Republican Senate hopeful in a race that had tightened unexpectedly.</p><p>Seoul offered a different account. South Korean Industry Minister Kim Jung-kwan said the Alaska announcement went “far beyond what was agreed,” and his ministry said it had yet to decide whether to invest at all, or how much. A U.S. Commerce Department fact sheet was also hedged, saying the two sides would begin work on the project provided it proved commercially reasonable and met domestic legal requirements.</p><p>Washington has also promoted a Texas project worth about $22 billion and a nuclear framework worth as much as $120 billion, but Seoul has signaled caution on both, with individual reactor projects still under review. The Diplomat said the election did not drive negotiations that have been underway for nearly a year, though the announcements clearly carry electoral value. On Sept. 30, Trump said he had done “a very bad job of explaining how good the country is doing.”</p><p>According to the analysis, the structure of the deals explains much of the confusion. The Japanese and South Korean arrangements are administrative and create no legally binding obligations, and no money is normally owed for at least 45 business days after a partner government is told the president has picked a project. South Korean law adds reviews of commercial viability, scrutiny by a government committee and reports to National Assembly committees before Seoul consults Washington.</p><p>Before the Alaska event, the U.S. side pushed for faster progress while Seoul stressed commercial logic, and the planned announcement had already been delayed over those differences. The analysis suggests the election may have handed Seoul temporary leverage, even though Washington remains much the stronger party, because delay becomes a bargaining tool when one side prizes speed. It adds that Tokyo and Seoul could use Trump’s urgency to seek better terms, though a direct link between the vote and announcement timing is hard to prove.</p><p>Japan’s pledge has moved faster. On Feb. 17, Trump unveiled an initial $36 billion tranche of Japan’s $550 billion commitment, and Tokyo signed its first financing agreement, worth about $2.2 billion, roughly 10 weeks later on May 1. By September, Japan had unveiled two rounds of projects totaling about $109 billion, which the article says shows a lag between announcement and financing is built into the process.</p><p>The Diplomat said a fresh Japanese package before Nov. 3 would bolster the argument that American electoral timing is driving the schedule, while an announcement held until the Nov. 18-19 APEC leaders’ meeting in Shenzhen would point to diplomatic scheduling instead. It concluded that the midterms are not reshaping Washington’s economic approach to Seoul and Tokyo but could make the administration eager to claim progress before any money arrives.</p>', 'An analysis in The Diplomat says Japanese and South Korean investment pledges are being showcased as quick wins before the U.S. midterms, while Seoul describes the deals far more cautiously.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/2646/3824994628_f378ee4ddd_b.jpg', '2026-10-13T18:32:00Z'::timestamptz, array['United States','South Korea','Japan','Investment','Midterm Elections']::text[]),
  ('central-asia', 'India Confirms Akash Air Defense Exports to Tajikistan and Turkmenistan', '<p>India has exported its domestically developed Akash medium-range surface-to-air missile system to Tajikistan and Turkmenistan, Indian Defense Secretary Rajesh Kumar Singh said in early October, The Diplomat reported. The sales would be India’s first defense exports to either Central Asian country.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/AKASH_Surface_to_Air_Missile_at_National_Military_Memorial%2C_Bengaluru%2C_Karnataka%2C_India_%28Ank_Kumar%2C_Infosys_Limited%29_04.jpg/1280px-AKASH_Surface_to_Air_Missile_at_National_Military_Memorial%2C_Bengaluru%2C_Karnataka%2C_India_%28Ank_Kumar%2C_Infosys_Limited%29_04.jpg" alt="An Akash surface-to-air missile on display at the National Military Memorial in Bengaluru, India."><p><em>An Akash surface-to-air missile on display at the National Military Memorial in Bengaluru, India. File photo. (Photo: Ank Kumar / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0)</em></p><p>In comments to Asian News International (ANI), Singh called the mobile Akash system a “success story” and said Armenia had been its first foreign buyer, acquiring several batteries, before the two Central Asian states followed. He said production had stabilized and that India’s own armed forces had placed further orders, adding that he expected more export interest from around the world.</p><p>Singh’s remarks confirmed a July report in BW Businessworld, an Indian business magazine, that drew on official data to name the two countries as destinations for the system. ANI said India’s Defense Research and Development Organization and the Ministry of Defense had both confirmed the new buyers, but neither Tajikistan nor Turkmenistan has addressed the reports publicly.</p><p>No details have emerged on how many systems the two countries are buying, when they will be delivered or what they cost. Armenia’s purchase offers a reference point: Yerevan signed a $720 million contract with Bharat Dynamics Limited in 2022 for an undisclosed number of Akash units and 15 Ashwin ballistic missile interceptors. After delays, it received its first battery, including four launchers and a Rajendra radar, in November 2024, and a second delivery was reported in July 2025.</p><p>Central Asian forces still rely mostly on Soviet- or Russian-designed equipment but have been adding Chinese, Turkish, Indian and Western hardware. RFE/RL’s Zamira Eshanova noted that Turkmenistan paraded a recently purchased Chinese FK-2000 air-defense system in Ashgabat on Sept. 27, along with a Brazilian A-29 Super Tucano and Italian Leonardo M-346 trainers, while Tajikistan has shown off China’s HQ-17AE. Uzbekistan displayed Chinese J-10CE fighters during its 2026 Independence Day events, and Kyrgyzstan has bought several types of Turkish drones in recent years.</p><p>India’s military ties with Tajikistan date to the 1990s and early 2000s and began with the refurbishment of Ayni Airbase, according to an October 2024 Oxus Society report. Dushanbe later turned back toward Moscow, dropping plans for India to base its own assets there and allowing Russian forces to use the facility instead. India has had access to the Farkhor Airbase near the Afghan border, though not a permanent presence.</p><p>The Diplomat said a single type of weapon will not fundamentally shift the region’s supplier balance once delivered. But it said the deals show Central Asian governments are seeking partners beyond Russia, a move that also brings challenges in making different systems work together.</p>', 'India''s defense secretary says the Akash surface-to-air missile system has been sold to Tajikistan and Turkmenistan, though neither government has commented and the terms of the deals remain undisclosed.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/AKASH_Surface_to_Air_Missile_at_National_Military_Memorial%2C_Bengaluru%2C_Karnataka%2C_India_%28Ank_Kumar%2C_Infosys_Limited%29_04.jpg/1280px-AKASH_Surface_to_Air_Missile_at_National_Military_Memorial%2C_Bengaluru%2C_Karnataka%2C_India_%28Ank_Kumar%2C_Infosys_Limited%29_04.jpg', '2026-10-13T16:48:00Z'::timestamptz, array['India','Tajikistan','Turkmenistan','Arms Trade','Central Asia']::text[]),
  ('defense', 'Pakistan Tests Longer-Range Fatah-IV Cruise Missile, Commentary Weighs Motives', '<p>Pakistan tested an extended-range version of its Fatah-IV ground-launched cruise missile on Oct. 6, and a commentary published in The Diplomat argues the system is aimed at countering India’s expected shift toward long-distance air-launched strikes. It was the third flight of the Fatah-IV, following a launch in May 2026, and the first in which the missile reportedly flew more than 750 kilometers.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Babur_Cruise_Missle_at_Ideas_2008.jpg/1280px-Babur_Cruise_Missle_at_Ideas_2008.jpg" alt="A Pakistani Babur cruise missile on display at the IDEAS defense exhibition in Karachi in 2008."><p><em>A Pakistani Babur cruise missile on display at the IDEAS defense exhibition in Karachi in 2008. File photo. (Photo: Skybolt101 at English Wikipedia / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0)</em></p><p>The commentary says the Fatah-IV grew out of the 2025 India-Pakistan clashes and was first shown publicly at Pakistan’s Independence Day event on Aug. 14, 2025. The missile is designed to hug the terrain to evade radar, can fly at up to Mach 0.7 and is armed with a 330-kilogram blast-fragmentation warhead. The new variant appears to have wings mounted beneath the fuselage and a slightly larger body for extra fuel, and the author estimates from imagery that its range could approach 1,000 kilometers.</p><p>The author’s first argument is that the Indian Air Force is likely to pull aircraft back to rear bases in any future clash, contending that India lost several jets in air-to-air fighting with Pakistan’s air force on the night of May 6-7, 2025. India has also approved a longer-range BrahMos supersonic cruise missile, reportedly reaching about 800 kilometers, the piece says. In the author’s view, Pakistan’s Taimoor air-launched cruise missile and other conventional weapons could not reach Indian installations deep inside the country, a gap the new missile is meant to fill by threatening radars and fighter fleets at distant bases.</p><p>A second rationale is geographic, according to the commentary. India’s major cities and commercial centers lie in the south and center of the country, far from the border, while Pakistan’s largest cities sit relatively close to India. The author argues that bringing Mumbai within range of Pakistan’s conventional missiles would discourage New Delhi from striking Pakistani cities in a limited conflict.</p><p>Third, the piece points to India’s air-defense buildup since what it calls the post-Operation Sindoor era, including additional S-400 orders, tests of the Kusha long-range interceptor and plans to fold these systems into Mission Sudarshan Chakra. Penetrating such a shield requires missiles that avoid radar detection, the author writes, a role the Fatah-IV is intended to play.</p><p>The commentary says Pakistan’s official policy rejects an arms race and presents the missile as a way to reinforce conventional deterrence, developed after what it describes as “unprovoked” Indian attacks on Pakistan in May last year. It also cites a recent statement by Indian army chief Gen. Dhiraj Seth about territorial victory in modern warfare as evidence of a hostile Indian outlook.</p><p>The author concedes that a single system cannot guarantee deterrence but argues that its continued development signals to New Delhi that installations deep inside India are no longer beyond the reach of Pakistan’s conventional missiles. The commentary does not include a response from Indian officials.</p>', 'A commentary in The Diplomat argues that Pakistan''s Oct. 6 test of an extended-range Fatah-IV is meant to hold distant Indian air bases and Mumbai at risk and bolster conventional deterrence.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Babur_Cruise_Missle_at_Ideas_2008.jpg/1280px-Babur_Cruise_Missle_at_Ideas_2008.jpg', '2026-10-13T16:00:00Z'::timestamptz, array['Pakistan','India','Missiles','Defense','South Asia']::text[]),
  ('south-asia', 'Pakistan Imposes Emergency and Governor''s Rule in PTI-Run Khyber Pakhtunkhwa', '<p>Pakistan’s federal government placed Khyber Pakhtunkhwa province under a state of emergency and governor’s rule on Oct. 11, seizing control of a region run by the Pakistan Tehreek-e-Insaf (PTI), the party of former Prime Minister Imran Khan, The Diplomat reported. Officials justified the step by pointing to a sharp increase in terrorist attacks and accusing the provincial administration of hampering counterterrorism operations.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/City_center_Peshawar_city.jpg/1280px-City_center_Peshawar_city.jpg" alt="The city center of Peshawar, capital of Khyber Pakhtunkhwa province, Pakistan."><p><em>The city center of Peshawar, capital of Khyber Pakhtunkhwa province, Pakistan. File photo. (Photo: Mohammed Ahmed Arsalan Khan / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0)</em></p><p>President Asif Ali Zardari approved the measure on the advice of Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif after a federal cabinet meeting. The government alleged that provincial authorities had failed to maintain public order and had given militant groups room to operate in a province long considered a PTI stronghold.</p><p>The government relied on two constitutional provisions, Articles 232 and 234. The first lets Islamabad declare an emergency when war or internal unrest beyond a province’s control threatens security, leaving the provincial assembly in place but giving federal law precedence. The second empowers the president to place a province under governor’s rule if its government cannot operate in line with the constitution, suspending its cabinet, transferring executive power to the federal government or the governor and letting parliament assume the assembly’s powers.</p><p>According to The Diplomat, the combined measures removed the PTI chief minister and cabinet and moved legislative and public order authority to the center, including powers to restrict street mobilization. Information Minister Attaullah Tarar said on Oct. 11 that the PTI-led government had formed ties with terrorist groups, alleging a “power-sharing formula” was taking shape between militants and the provincial government. PTI leaders reject those claims, saying security concerns are being used to justify a political move against the party and that federal authorities refused to consult them on militancy or allow them access to Khan, who is in prison.</p><p>The emergency was declared as PTI prepared a long march from the province to Islamabad to demand Khan’s release, and after the provincial leadership, notably Chief Minister Sohail Afridi, refused to back the federal counterterrorism policy. The ruling coalition, led by Sharif’s Pakistan Muslim League-Nawaz with prominent support from Zardari’s Pakistan People’s Party, has been locked in a bitter feud with PTI since taking office after a heavily disputed 2024 election.</p><p>Among its first steps, the federal government approved the immediate creation of an Area Development Secretariat, overseen by an Area Representative and Development Council, for the province’s border and former tribal districts. Its remit spans Bajaur, Bannu, Dera Ismail Khan, Khyber, Kurram, Lakki Marwat, Mohmand, Orakzai and Tank, as well as North Waziristan and both Upper and South Waziristan. The body is charged with restoring law and order, protecting residents and overseeing governance and development, bringing civil administration, police and military coordination under federal control.</p><p>Governor Faisal Karim Kundi said on Oct. 12 that the days of Islamabad and the provincial authorities failing to work together against terrorism had ended, declaring that “our policies will come from Islamabad … not Kabul, Jalalabad or Kandahar.” The Diplomat said the remarks signal a more forceful posture toward Tehreek-e-Taliban Pakistan in the border regions and a hard line with the Afghan Taliban on militancy, border management and cross-border sanctuaries.</p><p>For now, the federal government has imposed its authority over the province, The Diplomat wrote. The outlet said whether direct federal rule improves security will determine whether it wins public acceptance, and that Islamabad’s immediate test is to show results on the ground.</p>', 'Islamabad invoked two constitutional articles to take direct control of the province, citing surging militant attacks, while Imran Khan''s party says security is a pretext to curb its influence.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/City_center_Peshawar_city.jpg/1280px-City_center_Peshawar_city.jpg', '2026-10-13T11:42:00Z'::timestamptz, array['Pakistan','Khyber Pakhtunkhwa','PTI','Imran Khan','Counterterrorism']::text[]),
  ('trade', 'U.S. Freezes Green Card Sponsorship by Eight Tech Firms, Straining Ties With India', '<p>The Trump administration has suspended eight technology companies, among them several of India’s largest IT firms, from the program used to sponsor foreign employees for U.S. permanent residency, adding new friction to relations between Washington and New Delhi, The Diplomat reported. India’s foreign ministry responded with a sharply worded statement that also took aim at comments by Vice President J.D. Vance.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Infosys.Electronic.City.Lunch.Time.JPG/1280px-Infosys.Electronic.City.Lunch.Time.JPG" alt="The Infosys campus in Electronic City, Bengaluru, India."><p><em>The Infosys campus in Electronic City, Bengaluru, India. File photo. (Photo: Zondor at English Wikipedia / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0)</em></p><p>Labor Secretary Keith Sonderling said on Oct. 8 that Cognizant, Infosys, Tata Consultancy Services, Wipro, HCL Technologies, Capgemini, Microsoft and Adobe had been removed from the Permanent Labor Certification program, known as PERM, which tech and outsourcing companies rely on for green card sponsorship. “We will not accept any new or process any pending permanent labor certification applications involving these companies,” Sonderling said.</p><p>Indian firms played down the impact. Infosys said it did not expect a material effect, while the industry group NASSCOM said Indian companies had sharply reduced their use of H-1B visas and expanded hiring in the U.S., and that relatively few workers move from H-1B status to permanent residency through PERM. Ajay Srivastava, head of the New Delhi-based Global Trade Research Initiative, told The Diplomat the step was a warning to the sector, noting that the U.S. accounts for 54.1 percent of India’s software and IT-enabled services exports, which earned $221.4 billion there in 2025-26, up 8.2 percent.</p><p>Vice President J.D. Vance added to the tension, telling reporters that Microsoft needed to hire American workers and could not replace laid-off Americans with “foreign indentured servants.” India’s Ministry of External Affairs said talent mobility benefits both economies and that the U.S. measures do not serve the countries’ shared goals, calling the use of language with painful colonial connotations “deeply offensive.” The Diplomat said the reference recalled British colonial authorities shipping more than a million Indians abroad as contract laborers after Britain abolished slavery in 1833.</p><p>Some in India speculate the move is meant to show Trump’s domestic audience a tough stance on immigration ahead of November’s midterm elections. A bilateral trade pact remains unsigned after many rounds of talks, and Washington’s outreach to Pakistan has irritated New Delhi. In a September appearance at the Asia Society in New York, External Affairs Minister S. Jaishankar named the U.S. anti-immigration push as one of the main sources of strain.</p><p>The administration has also targeted the H-1B program, which issues 65,000 visas a year plus 20,000 for holders of advanced degrees. In August it proposed raising the fee for new H-1B visas to more than $100,000, up from $2,000 to $5,000, but a California court blocked the plan in September. India has long been the program’s largest beneficiary, and last year it took back almost 400 nationals deported by the U.S., whose arrival in military aircraft and shackles caused an outcry at home.</p><p>The PERM suspension is expected to come up when Secretary of State Marco Rubio, who also serves as national security adviser, visits India later this month. The agenda is also likely to include the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026, a law letting Trump penalize countries that buy Russian and Iranian oil; India, one of the largest purchasers of Russian oil, may seek clarification, since Washington is itself seeking to import Russian diesel.</p>', 'The Labor Department barred major Indian IT companies and others from a key permanent-residency program, and India''s foreign ministry objected sharply to remarks by Vice President J.D. Vance.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Infosys.Electronic.City.Lunch.Time.JPG/1280px-Infosys.Electronic.City.Lunch.Time.JPG', '2026-10-12T06:51:00Z'::timestamptz, array['India','United States','Immigration','Technology','Trade']::text[]),
  ('south-asia', 'Rahul Gandhi Leads Mass March in Delhi as Protests Over Election Chief Grow', '<p>Tens of thousands of people marched toward India’s Supreme Court in New Delhi on Oct. 12 behind opposition leader Rahul Gandhi, demanding the removal of Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar, The Diplomat reported. The march came after a weekend in which authorities imposed sweeping restrictions across the capital to stop demonstrations.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Rahul_Gandhi_2024.jpg/1280px-Rahul_Gandhi_2024.jpg" alt="Rahul Gandhi, leader of the opposition in India’s lower house, in 2024."><p><em>Rahul Gandhi, leader of the opposition in India’s lower house, in 2024. File photo. (Photo: Wikimedia Commons, CC0)</em></p><p>Gandhi, of the Congress party, sought the judiciary’s help over what he described as the election commission’s loss of integrity. Chief Justice Surya Kant declined to receive the opposition delegation, saying a meeting would breach the separation of powers, but opposition lawmakers, activists and ordinary citizens joined the march in large numbers.</p><p>Kumar, the first chief election commissioner chosen under a new law that lets the government select its preferred candidate, has faced criticism since a Special Intensive Revision of the electoral roll began last year and left millions of people off the rolls. Opposition parties accuse the commission of pro-government bias, including targeted manipulation of voter lists.</p><p>To block an Oct. 10 protest by the satirical youth group Cockroach Janta Party (CJP), which also wants Kumar to resign, police raided hotels, hostels, party offices and activists’ homes, The Diplomat said. Gatherings of five or more people were banned across large parts of the city, authorities deployed an estimated 30,000 police and paramilitary forces, dozens of Delhi-bound trains were halted or rerouted and internet access was curbed in central Delhi. Earlier CJP protests at Jantar Mantar in June and July led to the resignation of Education Minister Dharmendra Pradhan.</p><p>Thousands still took to the streets on Oct. 10, and police detained thousands, among them lawyers, journalists, legislators and four former opposition chief ministers. Protesters and police clashed in several locations, and many demonstrators alleged police brutality. A U.N. human rights spokesperson voiced concern, prompting the government to accuse the U.N. human rights office of bias.</p><p>A Supreme Court bench later told authorities to review their use of barricades, keep metro and rail services running normally and maintain internet access unless a shutdown is legally justified. By Monday, the government had shifted tactics, shutting entry and exit at six central Metro stations between 6 a.m. and 4 p.m., removing Jantar Mantar’s status as a protest venue and designating three other sites for demonstrations.</p><p>Protest leaders vowed to press on. “If Modi thinks taking away Jantar Mantar [as a protest venue] will put an end to protests, he is mistaken,” said CJP founder-convenor Abhijeet Dipke, who was among leaders released on Sunday, while opposition lawmaker Mahua Moitra said, “Nobody, but nobody, can stop us.” The ruling Bharatiya Janata Party accused anti-national and foreign-funded forces of seeking to destabilize the country, and BJP president Nitin Nabin said, “We will deal firmly with all such forces active outside or inside the country.”</p><p>The opposition INDIA bloc and the CJP plan further protests in the capital and other major cities, with nearly all opposition parties backing them. The Diplomat said the government has little room to maneuver, since Kumar is its own appointee and conceding could embolden the opposition.</p>', 'Tens of thousands marched toward India''s Supreme Court on Oct. 12 to demand the election chief''s removal, after a weekend in which police detained thousands and sealed off much of New Delhi.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Rahul_Gandhi_2024.jpg/1280px-Rahul_Gandhi_2024.jpg', '2026-10-13T08:14:00Z'::timestamptz, array['India','Protests','Elections','Rahul Gandhi','Narendra Modi']::text[]),
  ('southeast-asia', 'Malaysia to Press ASEAN Ministers on Haze Crisis Ahead of Manila Summit', '<p>Malaysia will raise the problem of transboundary haze at a meeting of ASEAN foreign ministers before the bloc’s 49th summit, which Manila will host next month, Foreign Minister Mohamad Hasan said, according to The Diplomat, citing the state news agency Bernama. Wildfires burning across Indonesia for four months, particularly on Borneo and Sumatra, have sent toxic smoke into Malaysia, Brunei, Singapore and parts of the Philippines.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/HAZE_strikes_Kuala_Lumpur_%287401600014%29.jpg/1280px-HAZE_strikes_Kuala_Lumpur_%287401600014%29.jpg" alt="Haze blankets the skyline of Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia."><p><em>Haze blankets the skyline of Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia. File photo. (Photo: Firdaus Latif / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 2.0)</em></p><p>Speaking to reporters in Sepang, Mohamad Hasan said the issue needed special attention because haze is not classified as a natural disaster under the bloc’s existing response mechanisms. “So, this will be one of the issues that I will personally raise at the meeting with my counterparts,” he said.</p><p>The fires, prolonged by an unusually harsh dry season, have closed schools across Malaysia. Last week the government shut schools in Melaka, Negri Sembilan, Selangor, Kuala Lumpur and Putrajaya, and the capital ranked among the most polluted major cities in the world. Last month, hundreds of schools in Sarawak on Borneo closed because of smoke from neighboring Kalimantan.</p><p>Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim said last week that he had raised the fires with Indonesian President Prabowo Subianto, who had “agreed to take the necessary steps.” Mohamad Hasan said Indonesia’s new foreign minister, Arrmanatha Nasir, told him that poor visibility was preventing firefighting aircraft from flying, and that many fires were burning far inland with scarce water or in peatland, where they are especially hard to put out.</p><p>Last month, Rizal Irawan, a senior Indonesian Environment Ministry official, said authorities were investigating 19 companies that may be linked to the fires. The Diplomat said the region’s recurring haze stems largely from agricultural burning on plantations of palm oil, maize, paper pulp and sugar, worsened by vehicle and industrial emissions and natural dry-season fires.</p><p>ASEAN members signed the ASEAN Agreement on Transboundary Haze Pollution in 2002, and in April the 11-nation bloc opened the ASEAN Coordinating Center for Transboundary Haze Pollution Control in Jakarta. But The Diplomat said such mechanisms lean heavily on consensus and national sovereignty, citing Rizky Ihsan of Griffith University, who argued the agreement was built to be acceptable to signatories and that the new center can act only when a national authority declares an emergency and requests help.</p><p>The Diplomat said ASEAN recognizes the need for stronger regional tools and that further discussion at next month’s summit could be a welcome, if belated, step. The outlet said that tackling the problem would require both closer cooperation among governments protective of their sovereignty and confrontations with entrenched business interests, and that without them the annual haze is likely to persist.</p>', 'Foreign Minister Mohamad Hasan says transboundary haze from Indonesian wildfires falls outside ASEAN''s disaster mechanisms, as smoke has forced school closures across Malaysia in recent weeks.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/HAZE_strikes_Kuala_Lumpur_%287401600014%29.jpg/1280px-HAZE_strikes_Kuala_Lumpur_%287401600014%29.jpg', '2026-10-13T06:55:00Z'::timestamptz, array['Malaysia','Indonesia','ASEAN','Haze','Environment']::text[]),
  ('korea', 'South Korea Recalls Envoy to Ukraine, Demands Apology Over POW Transfer Disclosure', '<p>South Korea decided on Oct. 8 to recall its ambassador to Ukraine, Park Ki-chang, escalating a dispute over Kyiv’s public disclosure that two captured North Korean soldiers had been transferred to the South, The Diplomat reported. It is Seoul’s first recall of an ambassador since 2008, when it withdrew its envoy to Japan over Tokyo’s territorial claim to the Dokdo islets.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/President_Lee_Jae-myung_2025_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-President_Lee_Jae-myung_2025_%28cropped%29.jpg" alt="South Korean President Lee Jae-myung in 2025."><p><em>South Korean President Lee Jae-myung in 2025. File photo. (Photo: Republic of Korea from Seoul, Republic of Korea / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 2.0)</em></p><p>The two soldiers were captured in January 2025 in Russia’s Kursk region, where North Korea had deployed troops to back Russia’s invasion of Ukraine, and reached South Korea in mid-September after saying they wanted to resettle there. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy revealed the transfer in a speech to the U.N. General Assembly 15 days before the recall. Seoul says both governments had agreed to keep it secret, while Kyiv initially denied any such deal before its Foreign Ministry blamed differing interpretations.</p><p>South Korean President Lee Jae-myung called the disclosure deeply regrettable in a Sept. 24 post on X, and Zelenskyy communications adviser Dmytro Lytvyn denied the next day that a confidentiality agreement existed. On Sept. 27, Lee’s office demanded an official explanation and apology, with senior presidential secretary Seong Ghi-hong saying Ukraine itself had asked for secrecy because it feared domestic criticism for passing up a chance to trade the soldiers for Ukrainians held by Russia. A day later, First Vice Foreign Minister Park Yoon-joo summoned Ukraine’s acting ambassador, Andrii Vieshkin, to repeat the demands.</p><p>Ukrainian Foreign Minister Andrii Sybiha called the dispute a diplomatic misunderstanding on Oct. 1 but did not issue the public apology Seoul sought. Lee wrote on X the following day that Kyiv had made South Korea’s head of state a “liar” by denying the arrangement. He also criticized Kyrylo Budanov, whom the source identifies as head of the Ukrainian presidential office, for warning on Sept. 27 that inter-Korean tensions were rising daily, shortly after suspected North Korean mines exploded in the Demilitarized Zone and wounded three South Korean soldiers, accusing Kyiv of speaking as if it wished for war.</p><p>On Oct. 6, Foreign Minister Cho Hyun told legislators that Ukraine had sent a message containing words of apology but fell somewhat short of Seoul’s expectations, while Ukrainian Foreign Ministry spokesperson Heorhii Tykhyi said a day later that Kyiv had expressed regret over the public criticism. Separately, Vladyslav Vlasiuk, Ukraine’s presidential commissioner for sanctions policy, said on Oct. 6 that fuel shipped from South Korean ports had helped Russia make up for refinery losses caused by Ukrainian strikes. Seoul’s presidential office rejected the claim on Oct. 7, and the government says petroleum products are not on its controlled export list.</p><p>In announcing the recall, South Korea’s Foreign Ministry said it hoped to resolve outstanding issues through continued consultations. Ukraine’s Foreign Ministry replied that diplomacy should prevail over emotions, and Tykhyi said efforts were underway through private channels.</p><p>According to Seoul, the soldiers’ families remain in North Korea and could face reprisals over the defections. South Korea’s National Intelligence Service told lawmakers on Sept. 30 that the disclosure appeared designed to build public support in the South for arms deliveries to Ukraine, which Seoul has aided financially and with humanitarian assistance but not with lethal weapons.</p><p>The Diplomat said Seoul has set three conditions: Kyiv must acknowledge that a confidentiality agreement existed, issue an official public apology and commit to measures preventing a repeat. Park may return to Kyiv only once all three are met, the outlet said.</p>', 'Seoul withdrew Ambassador Park Ki-chang after President Zelenskyy publicly revealed the transfer of two captured North Korean soldiers, a move South Korea says breached a confidentiality agreement.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/President_Lee_Jae-myung_2025_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-President_Lee_Jae-myung_2025_%28cropped%29.jpg', '2026-10-12T16:11:00Z'::timestamptz, array['South Korea','Ukraine','North Korea','Diplomacy','Prisoners of War']::text[]),
  ('trade', 'EU to Cap Chinese Hybrid Vehicle Imports at 350,000 a Year Under New Deal', '<p>The European Union will limit imports of hybrid electric vehicles from China to 350,000 a year, the European Commission has confirmed, The Guardian reported. The quota, part of a four-year agreement struck in Beijing last week, takes effect in December and is expected to cut China’s market share from 6 percent to about 3 percent.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Berlaymont_building_Brussels.jpg/1280px-Berlaymont_building_Brussels.jpg" alt="The Berlaymont building, headquarters of the European Commission, in Brussels."><p><em>The Berlaymont building, headquarters of the European Commission, in Brussels. File photo. (Photo: KenSwe72 / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>Brussels is imposing the cap under World Trade Organization safeguard rules, which allow it to act quickly without the lengthy investigations required for anti-dumping duties. Vehicles exceeding the quota will not face tariffs but will be held in customs at European ports. Chinese cars assembled inside the EU, such as in Hungary or, in the future, Spain, are exempt, officials said, while vehicles that EU carmakers build in China are covered.</p><p>Imports have surged over the past two years, with about 800,000 battery and plug-in hybrid vehicles expected to be sold this year. Chinese customs data show hybrid exports to the EU tripled to 6.7 billion euros in the first half of the year, from 2.2 billion euros a year earlier.</p><p>Under the deal, China also agreed to lift some tariffs on auto parts and halve duties on EU footwear, olive oil and ice cream. Officials said Beijing fully supports the quota and that talks continue on chemicals, renewable energy, furniture and other goods, with one official saying “this is by no means the end of the story.”</p><p>The agreement comes amid growing alarm over a trade deficit with China that The Guardian put at 1 billion pounds a day. Sources described a fundamental change in EU strategy, from trying to boost exports to China toward restricting imports, driven by fears of deindustrialization and job losses. Last month the EU sought feedback on a rule that could require companies to diversify supply chains, while separate “Made in Europe” legislation would apply the same principle to public procurement, and on Thursday EU leaders are set to discuss a French-German proposal for a so-called kill switch to block certain goods from the single market in an emergency such as a rare earths ban.</p><p>Andrew Small, director of the Asia program at what the paper called the European Council for Foreign Relations, said Beijing would probably try to draw the EU into prolonged negotiations on other sectors but that the political mood in Europe had shifted. “My sense is that things have now moved somewhat too far politically,” he said.</p>', 'The European Commission says the WTO safeguard quota, agreed with Beijing and taking effect in December, will roughly halve China''s market share as Brussels turns toward curbing imports.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Berlaymont_building_Brussels.jpg/1280px-Berlaymont_building_Brussels.jpg', '2026-10-12T14:23:55Z'::timestamptz, array['European Union','China','Electric Vehicles','Trade']::text[]),
  ('southeast-asia', 'Malaysia''s Anwar Hosts Myanmar''s Min Aung Hlaing, Backs Return to ASEAN Fold', '<p>Malaysian Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim hosted Myanmar’s military-appointed leader Min Aung Hlaing in Putrajaya on Oct. 8 and pledged to work toward Myanmar’s reintegration into the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, The Diplomat reported. The two-day visit is part of a diplomatic push by Min Aung Hlaing to shore up partnerships and normalize his country’s standing in the bloc.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/2025_Anwar_Ibrahim_%28cropped%29.jpg" alt="Malaysian Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim in 2025."><p><em>Malaysian Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim in 2025. File photo. (Photo: Lula Oficial / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0)</em></p><p>After an official welcome at the Perdana Putra Complex, the two leaders met one-on-one before talks between senior ministers and officials, according to the Malaysian state news agency Bernama. Min Aung Hlaing had also addressed Malaysian business leaders the previous evening, encouraging them to invest in Myanmar.</p><p>Anwar wrote on X that the half-hour meeting centered on the situation of Myanmar refugees living in Malaysia and the outlook for their “voluntary, safe and dignified return, regardless of ethnicity.” The two countries recently agreed to repatriate about 5,000 Myanmar nationals held in Malaysian immigration detention centers, and the first group of 1,476 left by ship on Sept. 29.</p><p>Speaking to reporters, Anwar said his government still had concerns about Myanmar but was prepared to respect differences, Reuters reported, and he appeared to back normalizing Myanmar’s ties with ASEAN, which has barred it from summits since late 2021 over its failure to implement the Five-Point Consensus peace plan. He said Malaysia would do what it could to eventually “take Myanmar as part of our community of nations, without prejudice.” Malaysia’s Foreign Ministry said the engagement was consistent with the consensus and followed an ASEAN decision at its last summit to keep engaging Myanmar.</p><p>Malaysia is the fifth ASEAN member to host Min Aung Hlaing since he became president in April, after Laos, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam, and the first from maritime Southeast Asia. The visit marks a notable change for a country that had been among the bloc’s harshest critics of the 2021 military coup, and Anwar said last month he needed a counterpart to negotiate the return of Myanmar nationals he said were in Malaysia illegally.</p><p>The issue has become politically pressing for Anwar amid rising public hostility toward Myanmar asylum seekers, especially Rohingya Muslims, large numbers of whom arrived after a 2017 military campaign in Rakhine State that The Diplomat described as ethnic cleansing and that drove more than 740,000 people into Bangladesh. The U.N. refugee agency’s office in Kuala Lumpur has about 126,000 Rohingya asylum seekers on its register, and Anwar’s government has called refugees a financial burden, a security risk and a cause of social discord.</p><p>Rights advocates criticized the visit. The Burmese Rohingya Organization U.K. noted that an Argentine federal court has issued an arrest warrant for Min Aung Hlaing over alleged genocide and crimes against humanity against the Rohingya between 2012 and 2017, and urged Anwar to have him detained, saying “he belongs on trial, not on a red carpet.”</p><p>At a joint news conference, Min Aung Hlaing said Myanmar would accept back only refugees confirmed to hold Myanmar citizenship. BBC correspondent Jonathan Head noted on X that this effectively excludes most Rohingya, whom successive governments in Myanmar have refused citizenship, raising questions about whether Anwar’s outreach will achieve its domestic aims.</p>', 'Talks in Putrajaya focused on repatriating Myanmar nationals, but Anwar also signaled support for Myanmar''s reintegration into ASEAN, a shift for one of the bloc''s most vocal critics of the 2021 coup.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/2025_Anwar_Ibrahim_%28cropped%29.jpg', '2026-10-08T08:04:00Z'::timestamptz, array['Malaysia','Myanmar','ASEAN','Rohingya','Refugees']::text[]),
  ('japan', 'Japan''s Non-Nuclear Principles Face Fresh Scrutiny Ahead of New Security Strategy', '<p>Japan''s long-standing pledge to keep nuclear weapons off its soil is returning to the center of political debate as Prime Minister Takaichi Sanae''s government drafts a new National Security Strategy, which is scheduled for completion by the end of 2026, according to an analysis published by The Diplomat. The article argues that a change in U.S. naval weaponry could soon force Tokyo to confront a question many politicians would prefer to defer.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Eisaku_Sato.jpg" alt="Former Japanese Prime Minister Eisaku Sato, who set out the three non-nuclear principles."><p><em>Former Japanese Prime Minister Eisaku Sato, who set out the three non-nuclear principles. Archive photo. (Photo: Cabinet Public Affairs Office, Cabinet Secretariat / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>The three non-nuclear principles, first set out by Prime Minister Sato Eisaku in 1967 and reaffirmed several times by the Diet, commit Japan to not possessing, producing or permitting the introduction of nuclear weapons. The analysis notes that even their standing is disputed, with some calling them a foundational national principle and others a policy guideline the government may alter. Because Japan belongs to the Non-Proliferation Treaty, the article says, only the third pledge, on non-introduction, is open to real debate.</p><p>The piece recounts that in March 2010, Foreign Minister Okada Katsuya disclosed a broadly defined secret understanding between Tokyo and Washington on the issue. Washington had read introduction as covering only permanent storage of warheads on land, while Tokyo also counted transit through its waters and port calls by nuclear-armed ships. According to the analysis, it is now widely acknowledged that by Japan''s definition the United States brought nuclear weapons into the country during the Cold War.</p><p>Since then, Tokyo has said the matter need not worry the public because U.S. surface ships do not carry nuclear weapons and the vessels that routinely visit Japan have no nuclear system. Successive governments have also kept Okada''s position that, if Japan''s survival truly depended on it, the sitting government "shall make a decision by taking full responsibility," leaving room for introduction in a crisis. Some therefore contend the policy has already been reduced to "two and a half" principles.</p><p>The article contends that this formula falls short. It covers only emergencies rather than peacetime, it ignores the U.S. sense of introduction, meaning storage on land, and it does not address the U.S. effort to field a nuclear-armed sea-launched cruise missile, known as SLCM-N, by the mid-2030s. The missile is expected, though not officially confirmed, to be deployed on attack submarines of the type that regularly call at Japanese ports.</p><p>Once those submarines are able to carry the weapon, the analysis argues, Tokyo will struggle to rebut claims that nuclear arms are entering Japan, since Washington''s neither-confirm-nor-deny policy means it will not say what any boat carries. Banning such visits, as some critics of the alliance may demand, could weaken deterrence and strain ties with the United States, the article says.</p><p>Short of restricting access, the piece lays out three paths: dropping or amending the non-introduction principle; explicitly permitting nuclear weapons, including storage on land, which could pave the way for nuclear sharing; or narrowing Japan''s definition so that transit and port calls no longer count, matching the U.S. interpretation. Any of these would be fiercely contested at home, the analysis concludes, and while many politicians appear to think they have time, it argues that groundwork for a decision should begin now.</p>', 'An analysis in The Diplomat argues that planned U.S. nuclear-armed cruise missiles on submarines could force Tokyo to revisit its pledge not to allow nuclear weapons into Japan.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Eisaku_Sato.jpg', '2026-10-10T23:16:00Z'::timestamptz, array['Japan','Nuclear Weapons','U.S.-Japan Alliance','Security Policy']::text[]),
  ('opinion', 'Writers Argue Kim Jong Un Stands to Gain From a Fourth Summit With Trump', '<p>North Korean leader Kim Jong Un has so far rebuffed President Donald Trump''s push for a fourth meeting, but an opinion piece in The Diplomat argues that some in Pyongyang may see such a summit as a major opportunity, though not for the reasons Washington might expect. The authors, who say they have spent decades studying North Korea and meeting North Koreans, frame their argument as the case the country''s Foreign Ministry might present to Kim.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/7854/47239200091_118eeeae15_b.jpg" alt="President Donald Trump during his 2019 trip to Vietnam for the Hanoi summit with Kim Jong Un."><p><em>President Donald Trump during his 2019 trip to Vietnam for the Hanoi summit with Kim Jong Un. File photo. (Photo: The White House / Flickr, Public domain)</em></p><p>In their telling, the ministry would tell Kim that North Korea now negotiates from strength, buoyed by its military and economic policies and its close ties with Russia and China, while the United States is weaker than during the summits of Trump''s first term. Rather than putting its nuclear program on the table as it did in Singapore, Hanoi and at the Demilitarized Zone, the authors write, Pyongyang would use a new meeting to bolster Moscow and Beijing, erode U.S. influence, nudge Trump toward accepting its arsenal and widen rifts in the South Korea-U.S. alliance.</p><p>The writers contend that Kim''s delay has already paid off, pointing to Trump''s shortening of one joint South Korea-U.S. exercise and what they describe as his drift away from the goal of denuclearization, which they say the State Department still backs. They add that the ministry would expect Trump to be even more eager to present any meeting as a personal win if Republicans lose the November congressional elections, an outcome the authors anticipate.</p><p>The piece offers a sharply critical portrait of the president that the authors attribute to North Korea''s private assessment, saying analysts in Pyongyang would see Trump, now 80, as more susceptible to flattery, more impulsive and weaker at strategic thinking than in 2018 and 2019. On substance, they predict he could float a nuclear freeze or revive his 2019 Hanoi idea of a moratorium on intercontinental ballistic missile tests, which South Korean President Lee Jae-myung also raised at the recent U.N. General Assembly. Kim could deflect such proposals, they argue, by insisting on a strong deterrent and demanding that any ICBM moratorium also bind other nuclear powers.</p><p>The authors argue that sanctions relief would carry less weight than in the past because North Korea has evaded restrictions with help from Moscow and Beijing and earns billions of dollars through illicit cyber activity, though lifting them could still raise its international standing. They also suggest that any such offer could divide Western members of the U.N. Security Council, with Britain and France expected to resist.</p><p>According to the piece, the ministry would urge Kim to stress his theme of "peaceful coexistence," press Trump to put any acknowledgment of North Korea''s nuclear status in writing, encourage a smaller U.S. military commitment to South Korea, and seriously weigh any offer of diplomatic recognition and an exchange of embassies. Such steps, the authors argue, would advance Pyongyang''s quest for acceptance as a nuclear power while unsettling Seoul and Tokyo.</p><p>Even a summit without concrete results would strengthen Kim''s standing abroad, the writers conclude, and a repeat of Trump''s walkout in Hanoi would damage Washington''s image more than Kim''s. They recall that after the 2019 Panmunjom meeting, Trump reneged on a promise to cancel a joint exercise, after which North Korea stonewalled negotiations and ended its missile testing moratorium, a precedent they suggest the ministry would cite in expecting any new deal to unravel within months.</p>', 'In an opinion piece for The Diplomat, longtime North Korea watchers imagine how Pyongyang''s Foreign Ministry might urge Kim to meet Trump to win concessions and unsettle U.S. allies.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/7854/47239200091_118eeeae15_b.jpg', '2026-10-09T18:06:00Z'::timestamptz, array['North Korea','United States','Kim Jong Un','Donald Trump','Opinion']::text[]),
  ('japan', 'U.S. Marine''s Murder Arrest in Okinawa Poses Early Test for New Governor Koja', '<p>Okinawa police early on Oct. 4 arrested a U.S. Marine on suspicion of killing a 39-year-old woman from Naha in a hotel and taking her belongings, a case that confronts the prefecture''s new governor with its most enduring political dilemma, The Diplomat reported. Lance Cpl. Devin Jacob Ballard, 20, who is stationed at Marine Corps Air Station Futenma, denies the allegations, and the Marine Corps has said it will cooperate with Japanese investigators.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Marine_Corps_Air_Station_Futenma_20160720.jpg/1280px-Marine_Corps_Air_Station_Futenma_20160720.jpg" alt="U.S. Marine Corps Air Station Futenma in Ginowan, Okinawa."><p><em>U.S. Marine Corps Air Station Futenma in Ginowan, Okinawa. File photo. (Photo: Suikotei / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0)</em></p><p>The killing prompted swift protests from the national government. U.S. Ambassador George Glass was summoned by Foreign Minister Motegi Toshimitsu, while Defense Minister Koizumi Shinjiro called in Lt. Gen. Stephen Jost, commander of U.S. Forces Japan. Prime Minister Takaichi Sanae denounced the incident on Oct. 5 ahead of her policy address to the Diet and called for full cooperation with the investigation, stricter discipline and effective steps to prevent further crimes.</p><p>The case comes days after Governor Koja Genta took office on Sept. 30, following a Sept. 13 election that ended 12 years of prefectural leadership opposed to moving the Futenma base to Henoko in northern Okinawa. Koja has sought warmer ties with Tokyo and, from Oct. 1, swapped the prefectural unit that had led resistance to the Henoko plan for an office focused on speeding the return of land used by the U.S. military. He has nonetheless conditioned his backing for defense needs on prior explanation and the understanding of local residents.</p><p>Koja''s protest letter, read aloud on Oct. 5 to U.S. Consul General Andrew Ou and Lt. Gen. Benjamin Watson, who leads the III Marine Expeditionary Force, said the repeated occurrence of such grave crimes showed U.S. efforts on discipline and human rights education were "completely ineffective." The governor demanded tougher steps to prevent crimes and a reexamination of the Status of Forces Agreement between Japan and the United States, known as SOFA. Watson offered condolences and promised continued cooperation, saying American forces were committed to being "good neighbors" and winning the trust of Okinawans.</p><p>Okinawa hosts roughly 70 percent of the land area in Japan set aside exclusively for U.S. military use. Police cleared 101 Penal Code cases involving U.S. service members, civilian employees and their families in 2025, up from 73 in 2024, figures The Diplomat notes reflect cases rather than convictions and do not establish a comparative crime rate.</p><p>Under the SOFA, Japan usually holds primary jurisdiction when off-duty personnel commit offenses against its citizens, though U.S. authorities may keep custody of a suspect they already hold until indictment. After three U.S. servicemen raped a schoolgirl in 1995, Washington agreed to consider favorably Japanese requests for handovers before indictment in murder and rape cases. Ballard, however, was arrested by Japanese police and remains in their custody, so the question is less about custody than whether current arrangements ensure enough accountability, information sharing and prevention, the outlet said.</p><p>The case also arrives as Japan''s defense buildup in its southwestern islands becomes more operational, with plans to store U.S. Army Typhon and HIMARS systems at Kadena and to send Marine NMESIS anti-ship and MADIS air defense systems to Ishigaki during the Keen Sword exercise in October. Koja has not yet taken a position on the storage plan, saying Tokyo has not sufficiently explained its rationale or duration. The Diplomat said anger over the killing could make individual decisions on facilities and exercises harder, even without reversing Japan''s overall defense policy.</p>', 'The arrest of a Futenma-based Marine in the killing of a Naha woman drew protests from Tokyo and Okinawa days after Governor Koja Genta took office seeking closer ties with the central government.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Marine_Corps_Air_Station_Futenma_20160720.jpg/1280px-Marine_Corps_Air_Station_Futenma_20160720.jpg', '2026-10-05T19:52:00Z'::timestamptz, array['Okinawa','Japan','U.S. Military','U.S.-Japan Alliance','SOFA']::text[]),
  ('nuclear', 'China''s Proposal for Nuclear Lab Visits With U.S. Revives a 1990s Exchange Model', '<p>China has floated the idea of reciprocal visits to nuclear laboratories with the United States, and an analysis published by The Diplomat argues the proposal could give the two powers a modest, practical test of nuclear transparency. The piece cites an Oct. 6 CNN report that the two countries are holding informal, early-stage discussions on such visits, an idea Chinese officials first raised with U.S. counterparts this year.</p><img src="https://images.rawpixel.com/image_1300/czNmcy1wcml2YXRlL3Jhd3BpeGVsX2ltYWdlcy93ZWJzaXRlX2NvbnRlbnQvbHIvZmw0ODAwMjM4NjI3My1pbWFnZS1rcDR2d3d5ei1remVtZWt6Zi5qcGc.jpg" alt="Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory in California."><p><em>Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory in California. File photo. (Photo: U.S. Department of Energy / rawpixel, CC0)</em></p><p>According to CNN, as cited in the analysis, the talks have covered national laboratories run by the U.S. Energy Department and their counterparts in China, and Washington is open to discussing visits to nuclear test sites as well. The subject did not come up at the leaders'' summit in September, CNN reported, where the two sides agreed to build "a constructive relationship of strategic stability on the basis of respect, fairness, and reciprocity."</p><p>The analysis points to a precedent in the U.S.-China Lab-to-Lab Technical Exchange Program, launched in the mid-1990s, which linked Energy Department weapons laboratories with Chinese bodies such as the China Academy of Engineering Physics. China hosted American scientists at its weapons laboratory and the Lop Nur test site, but the United States later withdrew an initial offer to receive Chinese scientists at the Nevada Test Site. The program stalled in the late 1990s as relations soured, notably after a 1999 congressional report accused China of stealing nuclear technology, and later revival efforts under Presidents George W. Bush and Barack Obama failed.</p><p>Beijing''s official stance emphasizes transparency of intentions while guarding national security, the piece notes. China''s 2026 NPT compliance report warned that "inappropriate transparency may also undermine strategic stability" and favored gradual steps, while also highlighting a March 2026 tour of the decommissioned 816 nuclear facility in Chongqing by diplomats and representatives from nearly 20 countries. The analysis cautions that such a visit does not show China would open an active weapons laboratory in the same way.</p><p>Visits to working laboratories, the analysis argues, could occupy a middle ground between policy statements and disclosures about forces, covering how weapons institutions organize arms control work, staffing and management without addressing warhead numbers, deployments or readiness. It cites Chinese expert Li Bin''s view that strategic nuclear dialogue could take place at several levels and involve diplomats, the military and weapons laboratories, with transparency serving as a tool for stability.</p><p>Such visits would not settle the two countries'' differences over nuclear strategy, the analysis concludes, but direct exchanges could correct misunderstandings and reduce overreactions. If Beijing and Washington can share meaningful technical information while still disagreeing over force size and arms control, it argues, the effort could give concrete form to the "strategic stability" both leaders have endorsed.</p>', 'An analysis in The Diplomat examines a reported Chinese idea for reciprocal U.S.-China nuclear laboratory visits, arguing it could offer limited transparency without touching warhead numbers.', 'https://images.rawpixel.com/image_1300/czNmcy1wcml2YXRlL3Jhd3BpeGVsX2ltYWdlcy93ZWJzaXRlX2NvbnRlbnQvbHIvZmw0ODAwMjM4NjI3My1pbWFnZS1rcDR2d3d5ei1remVtZWt6Zi5qcGc.jpg', '2026-10-09T15:50:00Z'::timestamptz, array['China','United States','Nuclear Arms Control','Diplomacy']::text[]),
  ('southeast-asia', 'ICC Judges Rule Former Philippine President Duterte Fit to Stand Trial', '<p>Judges at the International Criminal Court have found former Philippine President Rodrigo Duterte fit to stand trial on crimes against humanity charges stemming from his deadly anti-drug campaign, The Diplomat reported. The decision by Trial Chamber III was issued on Sept. 28 but only recently made public, and it rested on conclusions reached by an independent expert panel along with submissions from the parties, the court said in a statement.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Building_of_the_International_Criminal_Court_in_The_Hague.jpg/1280px-Building_of_the_International_Criminal_Court_in_The_Hague.jpg" alt="The International Criminal Court in The Hague."><p><em>The International Criminal Court in The Hague. File photo. (Photo: User Hanhil from the Nederlandstalige Wikipedia / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0)</em></p><p>The court said the expert panel unanimously concluded that Duterte can understand the charges and the purpose of the proceedings, follow them, grasp the evidence and the implications of entering a plea, and instruct his lawyers. The ruling answered a September 2025 defense petition arguing that he was unfit because of "cognitive impairment in multiple domains."</p><p>Duterte, 81, is scheduled to go on trial on Nov. 30 on three counts of crimes against humanity over extrajudicial killings during the drug war, the signature policy of his 2016-2022 presidency. He is accused of murdering at least 76 people and attempting to murder two others, with 19 of the alleged killings dating from 2013 to 2016, when he was mayor of Davao City.</p><p>His lawyers have previously argued that his health was frail and worsening in ICC detention, and in one filing cited his illness and "advanced age" as humanitarian grounds for interim release. Judges rejected those arguments in January, finding him able to exercise his rights in pre-trial proceedings. Duterte has been held in ICC detention since he was arrested in the Philippines in March 2025, and Pre-Trial Chamber I later confirmed the charges and sent the case to trial.</p><p>Philippine police put the drug war''s death toll at a minimum of 6,000, while independent estimates reach 30,000. Duterte looked thin and haggard at an in-person court appearance in September, and at a status conference that month Presiding Judge Joanna Korner said the chamber was "very anxious" for the prosecution to finish its case as quickly as possible so that he lives to hear a verdict, The Diplomat reported.</p>', 'The International Criminal Court found the 81-year-old capable of facing three counts of crimes against humanity tied to his drug war, clearing the way for a trial set to open Nov. 30.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Building_of_the_International_Criminal_Court_in_The_Hague.jpg/1280px-Building_of_the_International_Criminal_Court_in_The_Hague.jpg', '2026-10-09T07:25:00Z'::timestamptz, array['Philippines','Rodrigo Duterte','International Criminal Court','Human Rights']::text[]),
  ('us-policy', 'U.S. Opens Direct Talks With Myanmar''s Military-Backed Government, Reuters Reports', '<p>The Trump administration has begun talking directly with the government installed by Myanmar''s military, its first such contact since the 2021 coup, Reuters reported, in what The Diplomat described as a significant shift in U.S. policy. The Reuters report, written by Simon Lewis and citing seven current and former U.S. officials among others, said the contacts have centered on specific issues such as shutting down online scam centers but could broaden to include Myanmar''s rare earth deposits.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/Myanmar_Min_Aung_Hlaing_on_March_4%2C_2025_%28cropped%29.jpg" alt="Myanmar’s military leader Min Aung Hlaing in March 2025."><p><em>Myanmar’s military leader Min Aung Hlaing in March 2025. File photo. (Photo: State Duma of the Russian Federation / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>According to Reuters, Michael Vance, the State Department''s assistant secretary for intelligence and research, took part in talks with Min Aung Hlaing, the coup leader who later became president, in Naypyidaw on Sept. 15-16. Four sources told the agency the meeting was officially aimed at freeing detained American businessman Adam Castillo but also created an opening for further engagement. Castillo, who once led the American Chamber of Commerce in Myanmar, was arrested at Yangon airport in June on unclear charges.</p><p>Back-channel talks on Castillo''s release preceded the visit, and Reuters reported that Myanmar sought in return only "an official engagement at the right level with the U.S. government." The two sides had also met in Tokyo in June, with officials from the Treasury Department, the Department of Homeland Security and the Pentagon joining Vance, according to a source cited by the agency. Josh Kurlantzick of the Council on Foreign Relations separately wrote that sources told him of secret meetings in Japan before the Naypyidaw trip.</p><p>The talks aim to combat scam operations that have taken root in Myanmar since the coup and have cost Americans billions of dollars, Reuters reported, and U.S. officials also discussed access to the country''s heavy rare earth deposits. Most of those deposits lie in northern areas held by rebel forces including the Kachin Independence Army, and The Diplomat noted that parts of the administration have fixed on them as Washington seeks to reduce its reliance on China for critical minerals.</p><p>After the 2021 coup, Washington reimposed sanctions on senior generals and military-linked businesses and a year later downgraded its diplomatic presence to a chief of mission rather than a full ambassador. Reuters said Vance has also met the democratic opposition and ethnic armed groups, and the State Department told the agency that its dealings with Min Aung Hlaing''s administration do not weaken its ties with other groups.</p><p>Any deeper re-engagement is likely to draw criticism given Myanmar''s ongoing civil war, which has left more than 100,000 people dead and 3.8 million displaced, The Diplomat said. A U.N. Development Programme report found that more than two in three people in the country are in or at risk of "multidimensional poverty." Min Aung Hlaing, president since April, has pursued a regional charm offensive, most recently receiving full state honors in Malaysia from Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim, as he seeks Myanmar''s full return to ASEAN.</p><p>Kurlantzick argued that U.S. access to the rare earths "is hard to imagine" given their location near the Chinese border in areas where Beijing holds sway. He also warned that engaging the regime would signal to autocrats that cooperation pays off and would deepen the suffering of Myanmar''s people.</p>', 'The Trump administration''s first direct engagement with Naypyidaw since the 2021 coup has focused on scam centers but could extend to rare earth deposits, according to a Reuters report cited by The Diplomat.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/Myanmar_Min_Aung_Hlaing_on_March_4%2C_2025_%28cropped%29.jpg', '2026-10-09T02:36:00Z'::timestamptz, array['Myanmar','United States','Rare Earths','Sanctions','Diplomacy']::text[]),
  ('china', 'China''s Revised Mobilization Law Widens State Reach Over Private Firms, Analysis Says', '<p>China''s revised National Defense Mobilization Law, which took effect on Oct. 1, sharply widens the circumstances in which Beijing can mobilize civilian resources and the range of entities obliged to help, according to an analysis published by The Diplomat. The revision is the first major overhaul of the statute since its adoption in 2010.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/7440/15738716804_85e6438592_b.jpg" alt="The Great Hall of the People in Beijing."><p><em>The Great Hall of the People in Beijing. File photo. (Photo: IQRemix / Flickr, CC BY-SA 2.0)</em></p><p>The original law was widely seen as focused on Taiwan, providing a legal framework for responding to a declaration of independence, the analysis says. The revised version, it argues, extends wartime obligations to private organizations and lets the state requisition or expropriate whatever mobilization requires, making it relevant to foreign governments and companies in scenarios beyond Taiwan and even in peacetime.</p><p>The key change, according to the piece, is a definition of defense mobilization that covers threats to sovereignty, unity, territorial integrity, security and "development interests." The analysis argues that the last category has no clear limits and that the law sets no threshold for invoking it, so a blockade of the Strait of Malacca or the South China Sea disputes could plausibly qualify. A Chinese Defense Ministry spokesperson has said the law should not be overinterpreted and is consistent with comparable legislation in other countries.</p><p>The analysis highlights Article 7, which provides for a "data service support system" giving the state authority to gather and use data tied to mobilization, and Article 8, which promotes advanced technologies and mobilization forces in emerging fields such as AI. The law also requires stockpiling of strategic materials and security reviews of military supply chains, and it permits the state to requisition civilian premises, facilities, vehicles and equipment after mobilization is declared.</p><p>The piece argues the new trigger also matters for India, whose economic exposure to China spans capital goods, solar modules, electronic components, pharmaceutical inputs, and rare earths and magnets. It says the law is flexible enough to cover economic disputes with New Delhi, potentially serving as a formal extension of export restrictions in place since April 2025.</p><p>With listed sectors ranging from energy, chemicals and nuclear facilities to transport, telecommunications, network security, medical care, food supply, construction, municipal infrastructure and news media, the analysis says companies doing business in China must now check whether partners operate in any of them, not just whether they have military ties. It stresses that the law does not signal an imminent emergency but contends it is part of a broader effort to build a mobilization system that legitimizes economic coercion.</p>', 'An analysis in The Diplomat argues that the first major overhaul of China''s 2010 mobilization law, in force since Oct. 1, adds a vague ''development interests'' trigger reaching well beyond Taiwan.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/7440/15738716804_85e6438592_b.jpg', '2026-10-06T16:45:00Z'::timestamptz, array['China','Defense Policy','Taiwan','Military-Civil Fusion','India']::text[]),
  ('korea', 'South Korea Blames North Korean Mine for DMZ Blast as Pyongyang Rejects Talks', '<p>South Korea''s military concluded on Oct. 5 that a North Korean antipersonnel mine planted south of the Military Demarcation Line caused a Sept. 21 explosion inside the Demilitarized Zone that injured three South Korean soldiers, two of them seriously, The Diplomat reported. Pyongyang has denied planting the mines and rejected talks on the border, putting President Lee Jae-myung''s engagement policy under strain.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/8218/8381385512_ee8a39ce32_b.jpg" alt="The Korean Demilitarized Zone seen from the South Korean side."><p><em>The Korean Demilitarized Zone seen from the South Korean side. File photo. (Photo: Jirka Matousek / Flickr, CC BY 2.0)</em></p><p>Kwon Dae-won, deputy chief of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, said at a Monday press conference that the finding was based on a joint investigation with the U.S.-led United Nations Command and on scientific evidence. Both the UNC and the South Korean military judged that the mines had been in the ground for at least 12 months. The military said it had begun destroying North Korean mines near the site by detonating them and called on Pyongyang to apologize to the wounded troops and remove mines laid in violation of the Armistice Agreement.</p><p>The blast occurred about 10 meters south of the line during an operation to clear a route for a joint inspection of terrain changes that officials suspected were signs of North Korean mine-laying. Forensic tests found traces of TNT and a brown synthetic material matching North Korean plastic mines, and investigators found another unexploded North Korean mine nearby, leading the military to believe at least four were in the area. The line itself has become hard to discern, with only about 200 of the 1,292 markers placed in 1953 still intact, and Seoul''s Defense Ministry has acknowledged that UNC and South Korean baselines differ for roughly 60 percent of border coordinates.</p><p>Kim Yo Jong, sister of leader Kim Jong Un, issued a flat denial carried by the Korean Central News Agency on Sept. 30, saying, "Clearly speaking, our soldiers have never crossed the border." When Unification Minister Chung Dong-young proposed on Oct. 2 that the two Koreas meet to confirm and resurvey the line, she dismissed the idea the same day as a "wild dream and unilateral jargon," saying Pyongyang would set its boundary by force rather than through negotiation.</p><p>North Korea began fortifying the border in 2024, after Kim Jong Un declared in late 2023 that the two Koreas were hostile states, and its troops have since installed barbed wire and mines along the line. Seoul recorded more than 20 MDL crossings by North Korean troops in August alone, exceeding the 17 recorded in all of 2025, and Pyongyang has threatened swift retaliation if South Korean forces fire directly at its workers instead of firing warning shots.</p><p>The episode echoes an August 2015 incident in which wooden-box mines near Paju severely wounded two South Korean soldiers, prompting Seoul to resume loudspeaker broadcasts, an exchange of artillery fire and, eventually, a six-point agreement under which Pyongyang expressed regret. This time, Seoul has no loudspeakers on the border after the Lee administration removed them, and North Korea has refused both responsibility and dialogue. Lee, who pledged in August 2025 to restore the 2018 inter-Korean military agreement, said in an Oct. 1 Armed Forces Day speech that his government would keep pursuing practical steps to ease military tensions.</p><p>Critics in South Korea have questioned Seoul''s conciliatory stance, and the government has said it will take necessary measures once the facts are established, without specifying what they would be. On Oct. 3, North Korea fired a ballistic missile from the Wonsan area that the Joint Chiefs said flew more than 700 kilometers, while KCNA claimed the intermediate-range missile struck a target 1,000 kilometers away. Kim Yo Jong said the discrepancy exposed limits in South Korean and Japanese tracking, describing the missile as able to change course at low altitude and equipped with artificial intelligence.</p>', 'Seoul''s military concluded Oct. 5 that a North Korean mine wounded three soldiers inside the DMZ, while Kim Yo Jong denied responsibility and dismissed an offer to resurvey the border line.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/8218/8381385512_ee8a39ce32_b.jpg', '2026-10-05T19:38:00Z'::timestamptz, array['North Korea','South Korea','DMZ','Inter-Korean Relations','Missiles']::text[]),
  ('japan', 'Trump-Xi Summit Optics Stir Japanese Fears of a New Nixon Shock, Analysis Says', '<p>The China-U.S. summit held Sept. 23-25 unsettled Japan less for what was agreed than for how it looked, according to an analysis published by The Diplomat, which argues that the symbolism carries special weight in Tokyo because of history. Japanese media focused on Donald Trump and Xi Jinping referring to their countries as former allies against imperial Japan, and government officials reportedly voiced confusion internally.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/Prime_Minister%27s_Office_of_Japan.jpg" alt="The Prime Minister’s Office of Japan in Tokyo."><p><em>The Prime Minister’s Office of Japan in Tokyo. File photo. (Photo: 総理大臣官邸整備検討委員会 / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>Ahead of the summit, Tokyo prepared for a worst case, a concern the analysis links to Trump''s suggestion after his previous meeting with Xi that arms shipments to Taiwan could serve as a bargaining chip. Prime Minister Takaichi Sanae secured a brief meeting with Trump beforehand and said afterward that it was important for ties between Washington and Beijing to support international stability, including in Japan-U.S. relations, remarks the piece reads as a signal against any deal at Japan''s expense.</p><p>After the summit, Takaichi wrote on X that Trump had given her a "detailed explanation" of the talks and called two leader-level meetings in a single week a sign of the alliance''s strength. The Yomiuri Shimbun, citing several Japanese officials, reported that Trump praised Takaichi to Xi as a "strong leader" and urged Japanese and Chinese leaders to meet. The analysis also notes that Piero Tozzi of the America First Policy Institute, a conservative think tank close to Trump, has stressed that the administration still regards China as a strategic competitor.</p><p>The piece traces Tokyo''s sensitivity to the 1970s, when the Vietnam War and the two Nixon Shocks of 1971, Washington''s sudden opening to China and its unilateral economic measures, damaged Japanese views of the United States. Both moves came with little or no consultation with Tokyo, and the analysis argues that for Japan the lack of diplomatic courtesy mattered more than the substance. Polling cited in the article shows the share of Japanese who said they liked the United States dropped to 18 percent in 1973 from 33 percent in 1970.</p><p>Today''s anxieties differ, the analysis argues, because amid a tense security environment and a relative decline in U.S. power, Japanese worries center on abandonment rather than entrapment, and Japan now sees the United States as an economic opportunity rather than a rival. Still, it says the personalized nature of current U.S. foreign policy will keep alive fears of spheres of influence in the Indo-Pacific and of Taiwan being lost, making routine diplomacy with Washington essential. In the summit''s immediate aftermath, the piece concludes, Japan''s approach appears to be working.</p>', 'An analysis in The Diplomat says Tokyo has worked to reassure the public after the September China-U.S. summit, pointing to the lasting damage the 1971 Nixon Shocks did to Japanese views of America.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/Prime_Minister%27s_Office_of_Japan.jpg', '2026-10-05T18:16:00Z'::timestamptz, array['Japan','China','United States','U.S.-Japan Alliance','Diplomacy']::text[]),
  ('analysis', 'What North Korea Has Gained From Sending Troops to Fight for Russia, Analysis Argues', '<p>North Korea''s deployment of troops to fight for Russia has brought Pyongyang returns that far exceed its battlefield impact, according to an analysis published by The Diplomat. An estimated 15,000 to 20,000 North Korean soldiers have been sent to Russia''s Kursk region since October 2024, and roughly 6,000 have become casualties, according to South Korean and British intelligence estimates, and Ukrainian officials say tens of thousands more could be requested.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Vladimir_Putin_and_Kim_Jong-un_%282024-06-19%29_12.jpg/1280px-Vladimir_Putin_and_Kim_Jong-un_%282024-06-19%29_12.jpg" alt="Russian President Vladimir Putin and North Korean leader Kim Jong Un in Pyongyang on June 19, 2024."><p><em>Russian President Vladimir Putin and North Korean leader Kim Jong Un in Pyongyang on June 19, 2024. File photo. (Photo: Presidential Executive Office of Russia / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>The analysis argues the decision was driven by what Pyongyang could extract from a Moscow short of manpower and ammunition by mid-2024. In June 2024, Kim Jong Un and Russian President Vladimir Putin concluded a Comprehensive Strategic Partnership Treaty in Pyongyang containing a mutual defense provision, and troops followed within months. Analysts cited in the piece list payments said to total billions of dollars, food and energy supplies, and reported transfers of air defense systems, Su-27 and MiG-29 fighters, and help with satellite launches and naval reactor technology for submarines.</p><p>The most valuable gain may be combat experience, the analysis contends. South Korea''s National Intelligence Service has assessed that North Korean forces have gained live exposure to drone and electronic warfare and modern combined-arms fighting, and soldiers returning from the front have reportedly become instructors, a pattern documented by 38 North. Kim has also framed the mission as solidarity, praising troops for fighting "for the fraternal Russian people."</p><p>The piece argues the partnership has complicated rather than unified the informal alignment among China, Russia and North Korea. Beijing has watched with unease and has reportedly grown distrustful over sensitive Russian technology reaching Pyongyang without its knowledge, yet it has largely avoided criticizing either partner, while Kim has become less dependent on Chinese patronage than at any time in decades.</p><p>On the battlefield, the record is mixed, the analysis says. By some estimates, 40 percent of the first 11,000-strong contingent was killed or badly wounded within three months, and U.S. officials described "human wave tactics" as largely ineffective, but RAND researchers have warned against dismissing the troops as unsophisticated, and Ukrainian accounts describe elite Storm Corps units as motivated and increasingly skilled at countering drones. Ukraine''s military intelligence chief has estimated that North Korean shells and missiles make up roughly half of the munitions Russian troops fire on the front line.</p><p>North Korean units helped Russia retake Kursk territory seized in Ukraine''s August 2024 incursion but have largely stayed on Russian soil, allowing both governments to deny direct involvement in the invasion. The analysis concludes that the military contribution has been real but limited, while Pyongyang has secured upgraded air defenses, combat-tested doctrine, hard currency, food, fuel and a mutual defense treaty with a Kremlin now invested in its survival.</p>', 'An analysis in The Diplomat argues that despite heavy losses in Kursk, Pyongyang has won cash, technology, combat experience and new leverage over Beijing from its troop deployment to Russia.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Vladimir_Putin_and_Kim_Jong-un_%282024-06-19%29_12.jpg/1280px-Vladimir_Putin_and_Kim_Jong-un_%282024-06-19%29_12.jpg', '2026-10-05T15:56:00Z'::timestamptz, array['North Korea','Russia','Ukraine','Kursk','China']::text[]),
  ('ukraine-war', 'Analysts Say Russia Aims to Make Ukraine Unlivable After Snubbing Energy Ceasefire', '<p>Russia''s strikes on a string of Ukrainian power plants within hours of President Donald Trump''s Sunday announcement of an energy ceasefire show that the Kremlin has little interest in U.S.-led peace efforts, two analysts argue in a commentary published by the Atlantic Council. Writing for the think tank''s UkraineAlert blog, Maksym Beznosiuk and William Dixon contend that Russian President Vladimir Putin is instead preparing to escalate the war from the air.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Volodymyr_Zelenskyy_2025_%28cropped%29.jpg" alt="Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy in 2025."><p><em>Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy in 2025. File photo. (Photo: President Of Ukraine / Wikimedia Commons, CC0)</em></p><p>The authors describe a reworked Russian strategy that has taken shape in recent months, built around an expanded air campaign that exploits Ukraine''s thinning air defenses. Ballistic and cruise missiles, along with increasingly capable strike drones, are being used against civilian targets nationwide, they write, with the aim of creating a humanitarian crisis severe enough to force Kyiv to give up.</p><p>In their view, the reliance on air power amounts to an admission that Russia has failed on the ground. After more than four years of fighting, they argue, the Russian army has not broken through, and Ukrainian forces have even retaken ground in parts of the Donetsk region this year through an effort called Operation Vivaldi, which the authors credit with stalling Russian attempts to encircle a belt of fortified cities in the northern Donbas.</p><p>The analysts warn that the heaviest bombing may still be ahead as winter approaches. They cite Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy''s warning in early October that Russia has stockpiled as many as 700 ballistic missiles for winter attacks, as well as Russian documents that Ukrainian officials say they intercepted. According to those officials, the plans single out hydroelectric and thermal generating stations, water treatment sites, routes for importing electricity from the EU and the hubs that tie nuclear plants into the grid.</p><p>Beyond the energy system, the authors point to a surge in attacks by jet-powered drones, which are much harder to shoot down than older propeller models and have repeatedly shut schools, businesses and public transport in Kyiv since late summer. They also say Moscow has sharply increased strikes on railways, Black Sea ports, logistics hubs and fuel infrastructure since the start of 2026, and that recent missile attacks forced Ukraine''s largest steel plants to halt production, hurting exports and state revenue.</p><p>The article notes United Nations warnings that Ukraine faces its toughest wartime winter since the full-scale invasion began, and quotes the head of the UN development agency, Alexander De Croo, as describing a "race against the clock" to repair the power network. The authors acknowledge skeptics who say bombing civilians often backfires, observing that Russia''s previous winter campaigns spurred more Western aid.</p><p>Beznosiuk and Dixon urge Ukraine''s partners to move before winter sets in, prioritizing more air defenses, replacement locomotives and rail repair equipment, and industrial-scale generators that municipalities can use to keep critical services running through blackouts. They conclude that Putin has no intention of accepting a pause in the fighting and is likely planning the largest bombing campaign of the war.</p>', 'Two analysts writing for the Atlantic Council argue that Russia''s strikes on Ukrainian power plants after Trump''s energy ceasefire announcement signal a winter air campaign meant to break Kyiv.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Volodymyr_Zelenskyy_2025_%28cropped%29.jpg', '2026-10-13T23:57:43Z'::timestamptz, array['Ukraine','Russia','Energy','Air Defense','Analysis']::text[]),
  ('ukraine-war', 'Analyst Says Ukraine and Allies Must Deter Russia After Another Black Sea Truce Fails', '<p>Russia''s rejection of yet another proposal for a Black Sea maritime ceasefire leaves Ukraine, with allied backing, as the only force able to hold Moscow in check at sea, Yevgeniya Gaber argues in an analysis for the Atlantic Council. Gaber, a nonresident senior fellow with the think tank''s Turkey Program and a former Ukrainian diplomat, writes that keeping Kyiv''s maritime gains will require more help from Black Sea and transatlantic partners.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52329438949_902c58a8e7_b.jpg" alt="The port of Odesa on Ukraine’s Black Sea coast."><p><em>The port of Odesa on Ukraine’s Black Sea coast. File photo. (Photo: imo.un / Flickr, CC BY 2.0)</em></p><p>According to the article, Moscow in August dismissed as a "half-measure" a Ukrainian offer to halt strikes on civilian targets at sea, and later turned down a limited truce forwarded by Turkey and the United Nations, saying "piecemeal arrangements" would not move the sides closer to peace. Russian strikes on merchant vessels, ports and maritime infrastructure have grown in the meantime, she writes.</p><p>Gaber cites figures from Ukraine''s infrastructure ministry showing that by mid-September, 274 civilian vessels and 1,173 port facilities had been damaged or partly destroyed since the full-scale invasion began. She lists recent deadly incidents, including a Sept. 17 drone strike on a Tanzania-flagged ship that killed its captain, an Oct. 3 strike on a Liberian-flagged cargo ship in an Odesa-region port that killed a crew member, and a drone attack on a Turkish corn carrier in neutral waters that killed two people.</p><p>The analysis also describes spillover across the region. Romanian Foreign Minister Oana-Silvia Țoiu has said her country recorded more than 100 incursions by drones or drone fragments, and in August Romania destroyed an explosive-laden sea drone only a few hundred meters from Neptun Deep, an offshore gas project, an incident President Nicușor Dan blamed on Russia. Moldova''s Defense Ministry reported 44 airspace incidents in 2026 as of Sept. 24, compared with 17 in all of 2025.</p><p>Gaber argues that Ukraine, despite lacking a conventional navy, has moved the contest away from Russian dominance at sea toward Ukraine''s ability to deny Russia free use of it, relying on AI-enabled robotic systems and uncrewed platforms that have left Russia''s Black Sea Fleet largely confined to port. She points to a Sept. 12 clash in which, according to Ukraine''s navy, a Sargan 3000 uncrewed surface vessel destroyed a Russian uncrewed boat in the first battle between naval drones. Yet thin air defenses, including shortages of air-to-air missiles for F-16s and of interceptors, still leave Ukrainian ports and shipping exposed, she writes.</p><p>Other coastal states cannot close the gap on their own, she contends. Romania and Bulgaria lack key coastal defense, radar and sensor capabilities, while Turkey, though far better armed, balances support for Kyiv with careful management of its ties with Moscow. She describes a Black Sea Maritime Security Hub proposed by the EU as still largely a concept and says the Mine Countermeasures Task Group run by Turkey, Romania and Bulgaria is present only two months a year and excludes Ukraine.</p><p>Among her recommendations, Gaber calls for stronger Ukrainian air and missile defense around ports, turning the mine countermeasures group into a standing patrol mission that includes Ukraine, and better air defense and surveillance for Romania, Bulgaria and Moldova. She also urges the EU, the United States and other G7 partners to answer every Russian attack on civilian shipping with more aid for Ukraine, tougher sanctions and added pressure on Russia''s shadow fleet, and argues that U.S. political backing can deter Moscow without large-scale American deployments.</p>', 'An Atlantic Council analysis argues that only Ukraine, backed by its partners, can deter Russia in the Black Sea after Moscow again rejected a maritime ceasefire as attacks on shipping rise.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52329438949_902c58a8e7_b.jpg', '2026-10-09T15:36:47Z'::timestamptz, array['Black Sea','Russia','Ukraine','Turkey','Maritime Security']::text[]),
  ('europe', 'European Embassies Vow to Stay in Kyiv After Russia Urges Diplomats to Leave Ukraine', '<p>More than 20 European Union embassies in Kyiv have publicly rejected a Russian call for foreign diplomats to leave Ukraine, posting the message "We are staying" on social media, according to the Atlantic Council''s UkraineAlert blog. Russia issued a statement on Oct. 3 urging diplomatic missions to exit the country immediately, repeating a similar appeal from May, as its bombardment of Kyiv and other Ukrainian cities intensified.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/4050/4652533569_8f304f00b5_b.jpg" alt="Independence Square (Maidan Nezalezhnosti) in central Kyiv."><p><em>Independence Square (Maidan Nezalezhnosti) in central Kyiv. File photo. (Photo: neiljs / Flickr, CC BY 2.0)</em></p><p>Many of the embassies accused Moscow of trying to intimidate the diplomatic corps and discourage support for Kyiv. Poland''s Ministry of Foreign Affairs said Russia''s renewed threats showed that "the Kremlin is trying to bully Europe and undermine our support for Ukraine."</p><p>EU Ambassador to Ukraine Katarina Mathernova said all 25 member states that have embassies in Kyiv would remain. "Russia has made such threats several times already," she said, adding that she viewed the warnings as a sign of frustration over Russia''s lack of success on the battlefield.</p><p>In the article, UkraineAlert editor Peter Dickinson argues that the renewed demand is part of a wider Kremlin effort to isolate Ukraine, now focused on Europe, which Moscow views as Kyiv''s chief backer. European officials accuse Russia of waging a hybrid campaign that stays below the threshold of a military response, including cyberattacks, sabotage, disinformation, political meddling and violations of airspace, he writes. Dickinson also points to Putin''s recent nuclear warnings over a purported NATO threat to Kaliningrad, the Russian enclave.</p><p>Dickinson contends that Putin is betting European support will erode as pro-Kremlin parties gain ground in countries including France and Germany, at a time when U.S. aid to Ukraine has already dwindled to minimal levels. He writes that Russia''s air offensive with ballistic missiles and jet drones is expected to reach its height in the weeks ahead as temperatures fall, which could leave millions of Ukrainians without heat, electricity and water.</p><p>While calling the diplomats'' stand a timely morale boost for Ukrainians, Dickinson argues it is unlikely on its own to deter the Kremlin. European leaders have increasingly denounced Moscow''s tactics but have not yet imposed tangible costs on Russia, a reluctance he says damages Europe''s credibility.</p>', 'More than 20 EU embassies in Kyiv posted "We are staying" after Russia urged foreign diplomats to leave Ukraine, a show of solidarity that an Atlantic Council editor says will not deter Moscow alone.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/4050/4652533569_8f304f00b5_b.jpg', '2026-10-06T23:55:45Z'::timestamptz, array['Ukraine','Russia','European Union','Diplomacy','Kyiv']::text[]),
  ('europe', 'Ukrainian Suspect Goes on Trial in Germany Over 2022 Nord Stream Pipeline Blasts', '<p>A Ukrainian man accused of sabotaging the Nord Stream pipelines that carried Russian gas to Germany is due to go on trial in Hamburg on Oct. 14, the first suspect to face a court over the 2022 sabotage, the BBC reported. German prosecutors, who identify him as Serhii K, allege he conspired with fellow Ukrainian military personnel to wreck the pipelines in order to deprive Russia of gas revenue for its war.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/48968624797_d8164ff94a_b.jpg" alt="The Hanseatic Higher Regional Court in Hamburg, Germany."><p><em>The Hanseatic Higher Regional Court in Hamburg, Germany. File photo. (Photo: Andrew Milligan sumo / Flickr, CC BY 2.0)</em></p><p>The suspect, named in court documents as Serhii Kuznetsov, denies the charges. He was arrested in August 2025 on a European arrest warrant after checking into a hotel near Rimini, on Italy''s Adriatic coast, and was handed over to Germany late last year after weeks of legal challenges. Prosecutors allege he was a serving Ukrainian army officer in 2022 and led a team made up of an explosives expert, a boat captain and professional divers.</p><p>Explosions on Sept. 26, 2022, destroyed three of the four pipelines in the Baltic Sea near Bornholm island, seven months after Russia launched its full-scale invasion of Ukraine. Western suspicion initially pointed toward Moscow, while Russian President Vladimir Putin blamed the United States and Britain, which denied involvement.</p><p>After Denmark and Sweden closed their investigations, German prosecutors turned their attention to deep-sea divers who had hired a yacht named Andromeda in the port of Rostock, and later said traces of underwater explosives were found on the boat. In January 2025, Germany''s federal court of justice ruled that the case fell under German jurisdiction. Prosecutors are pursuing it as a war crime under the German code covering crimes against international law, describing the blasts as a military attack on civilian objects.</p><p>The case is sensitive for Berlin, one of Kyiv''s closest allies. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy has said Ukraine had "nothing to do with" the blasts, but the indictment alleges the saboteurs acted "by order of Ukrainian state agencies." Oleksandr Merezhko, who chairs the Ukrainian parliament''s foreign affairs committee, told the BBC that Kyiv had no confirmation of the German account, calling it "one of the versions," and argued that Nord Stream 2 had served Russia''s preparations for war.</p><p>A second suspect, Volodymyr Zhuravlov, also denies the charges. A Polish court ordered his release in October 2025, with the judge finding the attack was not unlawful, but he was later arrested in Croatia, which approved his extradition last month. It is not clear when he will be transferred or whether his case could be joined to the Hamburg trial.</p><p>Stefan Meister of the German Council on Foreign Relations told the BBC that successive German chancellors have tried to keep politics out of the case and leave it to the judiciary. Merezhko said he did not expect the trial to damage relations, arguing that continued support for Ukraine is in Germany''s national interest.</p>', 'A Ukrainian man accused of sabotaging Russia''s Nord Stream gas pipelines is due in a Hamburg court, the first suspect tried over the 2022 blasts in a case that tests ties between Berlin and Kyiv.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/48968624797_d8164ff94a_b.jpg', '2026-10-14T02:42:22Z'::timestamptz, array['Nord Stream','Germany','Ukraine','Sabotage','Courts']::text[]),
  ('intelligence', 'MI5 Chief Says State Threats to Britain Have Quadrupled, Naming Russia and Iran', '<p>MI5 is now devoting more resources to countering hostile states than it did during the Cold War, the head of Britain''s domestic intelligence service, Sir Ken McCallum, said in his annual speech in London, warning that such threats had quadrupled over the past decade. He named Russia as "aggressor number one" and pointed to rising Iran-linked hostility toward Jewish sites and military bases, the BBC reported.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/69/Thames_House_-_Millbank_Entrance_-_London.jpg" alt="Thames House in London, headquarters of Britain’s MI5 security service."><p><em>Thames House in London, headquarters of Britain’s MI5 security service. File photo. (Photo: Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0)</em></p><p>McCallum said Russia was waging hybrid warfare to unsettle European countries while avoiding conventional conflict with NATO, even as its progress in Ukraine remains minimal and extremely costly. He said almost 1,000 expulsions since the invasion had gutted the Kremlin''s covert presence, leaving it scrambling to pay criminals to act on its behalf. "These are not the actions of a great power with the strategic upper hand," he said.</p><p>Asked about recent remarks by Prime Minister Andy Burnham that Russia had meddled in the Brexit referendum, McCallum said Moscow''s attempts were not news but drew a distinction between attempted interference and outcome achieved, stressing that the 2016 result was "not in dispute." He also warned against exaggerating the reach of Russian trolls and bot farms in a way that would help adversaries.</p><p>On Iran, McCallum said there had been 50 arrests over the past year linked to Iranian activity in Britain, much of it aimed at Jewish communities and at harder targets such as military sites. He told reporters that a recent incident at RAF Fairford in Gloucestershire, where three suspicious vans were seen near the air base and seven people were arrested and bailed by counterterrorism police, was not unique. Burnham has said Iran''s Islamic Revolutionary Guard was almost certainly behind it. He added that Britain''s National Security Act, a law from 2023 aimed at people working for foreign powers against British interests, is in heavy use against threats from Russia, Iran and China.</p><p>Turning to artificial intelligence, he said MI5 had caught terrorists trying to use AI to improve reconnaissance and attack planning, including with AI-enabled drones, and that the technology can produce propaganda tailored to individuals. He described extremists using AI to develop bioweapons as the gravest risk from misuse of the technology, though one not yet realized, and announced the launch of an MI5 AI Threat Assessment Centre.</p><p>McCallum said counterterrorism and state-threat work were now roughly equal in scale, which he called a major change. Britain raised its terrorism threat level to severe in April for the first time in five years, and he said the heightened risk came from Islamist groups such as al-Qaida and the Islamic State group and, in significant part, from extreme right-wing terrorism, which tends to involve young people. He said MI5 had recently investigated a radicalized 13-year-old in Britain, work that helped an international partner prevent a deadly attack.</p>', 'Sir Ken McCallum said MI5 now spends more on countering hostile states than during the Cold War, citing Russian hybrid attacks, Iran-linked plots and terrorists'' growing use of AI.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/69/Thames_House_-_Millbank_Entrance_-_London.jpg', '2026-10-13T18:52:15Z'::timestamptz, array['MI5','United Kingdom','Russia','Iran','Artificial Intelligence']::text[]),
  ('ukraine-war', 'Russian Missile Strike Hits Key Kyiv Power Plant Days After Trump Ceasefire Announcement', '<p>A Russian ballistic missile attack late on Oct. 13 struck a major plant supplying Kyiv with heating and electricity, as well as a residential building, Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy said, two days after U.S. President Donald Trump said Russia and Ukraine had agreed to an energy ceasefire, the BBC reported.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Destructions_in_Kyiv_after_Russian_attack%2C_2025-08-28_%2870%29.jpg" alt="Damage in Kyiv after a Russian attack on Aug. 28, 2025."><p><em>Damage in Kyiv after a Russian attack on Aug. 28, 2025. File photo. (Photo: Kyiv City State Administration / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>Kyiv Mayor Vitali Klitschko said two people were killed and 21 injured, four of them seriously, when the missile hit the apartment block. Zelenskyy said the strike on the Darnytska CHP-4 plant, also known as CHPP-4, came with "no early warning from our partners."</p><p>"Russia is targeting yet another power plant and the lives of our people," Zelenskyy wrote on social media, noting that Ukraine had backed an energy ceasefire. He called on the world not to stay silent and said it was important for the United States to see who is responsible for the war continuing.</p><p>It was the second straight night of Russian missile strikes on the capital. After attacks on the night of Oct. 12, there was speculation that Washington had cut Ukraine''s access to intelligence used to track incoming threats, which Trump denied earlier on Oct. 13. "We are helping them and we are sharing intel," he said.</p><p>Asked about Russian attacks on Ukrainian energy sites, Trump said Ukraine had hit many sites the day before, and he declined to say whether Russia had broken the ceasefire. Trump said both sides want the war to end but do not know how to get there.</p><p>After Trump''s announcement on Oct. 11, Russian Foreign Minister Sergey Lavrov said Ukraine did not deserve the respite a ceasefire would bring and argued that Europe would use any pause to supply Kyiv with new weapons. Zelenskyy said the same day that a 60-day ceasefire would allow Ukraine to hold presidential elections, following Trump''s remark that it was time for Ukraine to have a new president.</p><p>CHPP-4, which serves the Dniprovskyi district as well as part of the Darnytskyi district, has been struck several times during the war. A Russian missile attack in February forced it to shut down and left more than 1,000 residential buildings, 47 schools, 72 kindergartens and 18 medical facilities without heating and hot water, according to Reuters figures cited by the BBC.</p>', 'A Russian ballistic missile attack hit a plant supplying Kyiv with heat and power and an apartment block, killing two, Ukrainian officials said, two days after Trump announced an energy ceasefire.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Destructions_in_Kyiv_after_Russian_attack%2C_2025-08-28_%2870%29.jpg', '2026-10-14T04:56:57Z'::timestamptz, array['Ukraine','Russia','Kyiv','Energy','Ceasefire']::text[]),
  ('intelligence', 'Russia Detains Japanese Journalist on Sakhalin Island on Espionage Allegations', '<p>Russian authorities have detained a Japanese journalist on Sakhalin, an island in the country''s far east, and accused him of spying, Moscow said on Oct. 13, according to The Guardian. Foreign Ministry spokeswoman Maria Zakharova identified him as Yuki Imai of the Hokkaido Shimbun newspaper, saying the reporter held ministry accreditation but stood accused of "conducting espionage activities on Russian territory."</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk_overview.jpg/1280px-Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk_overview.jpg" alt="Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk, the main city on Russia’s Sakhalin Island."><p><em>Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk, the main city on Russia’s Sakhalin Island. File photo. (Photo: Maarten / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0)</em></p><p>Russia''s FSB security service said a Japanese national, whom it did not name, had gathered information on Russian defenses on behalf of Japanese intelligence. The agency released footage in which masked officers burst into a hotel room, push a man to the floor and handcuff him; the man was then taken by air to Moscow, and a court there ordered him held in pretrial detention. Russian media reported he would stay in custody until Dec. 12, and the charges are punishable by up to 20 years.</p><p>Zakharova said it was regrettable that Tokyo would try to use its correspondents in Russia to collect information harmful to Russian security, alleging this was "not an isolated case." Japan''s embassy in Moscow told the Kyodo news agency that the government was trying to confirm reports of the detention. The FSB gave no date for the arrest or the court hearing.</p><p>Russia classifies Japan as an unfriendly country because it joined Western sanctions over the war in Ukraine, and in recent years has arrested several citizens of such countries on accusations of working for Western intelligence. U.S. reporter Evan Gershkovich was detained on espionage charges in 2023 and given a 16-year prison sentence before his release in a 2024 prisoner exchange.</p><p>Moscow and Tokyo have disputed for decades the status of a chain of Pacific islands, known in Russia as the Kurils and in Japan as the Northern Territories, which the Soviet Union seized at the end of World War II. Russian President Vladimir Putin''s visit to one of the islands in August caused an uproar in Japan, and Tokyo has said it will continue to enforce sanctions on Russia and support Ukraine.</p>', 'Russia''s FSB said it arrested a Japanese national on Sakhalin for allegedly gathering defense information for Tokyo; the Foreign Ministry named him as Hokkaido Shimbun reporter Yuki Imai.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk_overview.jpg/1280px-Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk_overview.jpg', '2026-10-14T04:05:25Z'::timestamptz, array['Russia','Japan','Espionage','Press Freedom']::text[]),
  ('europe', 'Burnham to Host Zelenskyy at Downing Street as UK Speeds Up Missile Deliveries', '<p>British Prime Minister Andy Burnham is set to host Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy at Downing Street on Oct. 14, a visit intended to underline British support for Kyiv as Zelenskyy faces renewed friction with U.S. President Donald Trump, The Guardian reported.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Official_portrait_of_Andy_Burnham_MP_2026_%28cropped_3x4%29_2.jpg/1280px-Official_portrait_of_Andy_Burnham_MP_2026_%28cropped_3x4%29_2.jpg" alt="British Prime Minister Andy Burnham."><p><em>British Prime Minister Andy Burnham. Official portrait. (Photo: House of Commons / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0)</em></p><p>To coincide with the visit, Downing Street said it would speed up part of an ongoing 1.6 billion-pound package to deliver more than 5,000 British-made lightweight multirole missiles, or LMMs, to help Ukraine fend off Russia''s new generation of jet-powered drones. Those drones fly faster and higher than the propeller-driven Shahed models Russia used before, making them much harder to shoot down and allowing Russia to hit more targets, including in Kyiv.</p><p>Burnham''s government will also push for a full international ban on maritime services for exports of Russian liquefied natural gas. The chancellor, John Healey, will separately press other countries to ban Russian LNG during meetings at the International Monetary Fund summit in Bangkok, the newspaper said.</p><p>In a statement ahead of the meeting, Burnham described Britain''s backing for Ukraine as "rock-solid" and said it was vital for British security and for equipping troops on the front lines. His message to Ukrainians, he said, was that "we are with you for as long as it takes," adding that Russian aggression must not be allowed to succeed in Europe.</p><p>The two leaders have met twice since Burnham became prime minister in July: Zelenskyy visited a naval base in Portsmouth that month, and Burnham traveled to Kyiv in August. British governments have previously welcomed Zelenskyy after tense episodes with Trump, including a Downing Street meeting with Keir Starmer the day after Trump and Vice President JD Vance berated him in public in the Oval Office 18 months ago.</p><p>The latest visit comes as Ukraine faces stepped-up Russian missile and drone attacks and follows a series of Trump moves that have unsettled Kyiv, including a deal to buy Russian diesel, a call for new leadership in Kyiv and the announcement of an energy-related ceasefire that Zelenskyy said had come as news to him. Zelenskyy has voiced anger over the diesel deal, saying Russia''s subsequent strikes on civilian targets amounted to a "thanks" from Russian President Vladimir Putin.</p>', 'Britain''s prime minister will welcome the Ukrainian president to No. 10 and accelerate delivery of lightweight missiles to counter Russian jet drones, as Zelenskyy''s ties with Trump grow strained.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Official_portrait_of_Andy_Burnham_MP_2026_%28cropped_3x4%29_2.jpg/1280px-Official_portrait_of_Andy_Burnham_MP_2026_%28cropped_3x4%29_2.jpg', '2026-10-14T00:30:35Z'::timestamptz, array['United Kingdom','Ukraine','Diplomacy','Air Defense','Sanctions']::text[]),
  ('europe', 'More Than 160,000 March as French School Protests Enter Third Week, Joined by Thunberg', '<p>More than 160,000 high school pupils, university students and trade unionists marched in cities across France on Oct. 13 as protests over the state of public schools entered a third week, The Guardian reported. The interior ministry put nationwide turnout at 161,500, after marches drew more than 260,000 people on Oct. 6 and 75,000 the following Thursday.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52682884499_fe596982f3_b.jpg" alt="High school students march in Paris during a protest in February 2023."><p><em>High school students march in Paris during a protest in February 2023. File photo. (Photo: Paola Breizh / Flickr, CC BY 2.0)</em></p><p>Demonstrators are protesting teacher shortages, overcrowded classrooms and dilapidated school buildings. Most of the marches, including in Lyon, Lille and Marseille, were peaceful, but riot police fired tear gas as rallies ended in Nantes and in Paris near Montparnasse.</p><p>Swedish climate activist Greta Thunberg, 23, joined an early-morning demonstration at the Lycée Paul Éluard in Saint-Denis, a Paris suburb, where lawyers wearing their robes linked arms in a human chain to shield students following allegations of police brutality. "Young people are not being included in the conversation," Thunberg said. Students there said she encouraged them to keep their demonstrations peaceful.</p><p>Education Minister Édouard Geffray said 214 of France''s 3,700 high schools were fully closed on Tuesday and that 3,000 substitute teachers were being sent to schools with the worst staff shortages. French media reported cancelled or disrupted classes at many more schools, some of which have moved partly or entirely online, and an estimated 90 percent of universities were disrupted on Tuesday, according to the report.</p><p>Government figures show more than 6,800 people, mostly minors, have been detained during the protests, and the justice ministry said about 1,500 are being prosecuted. Twenty-four allegations of police brutality are under investigation, and injuries have been reported among more than 200 teenagers and 100 school staff members, as well as 700 police officers. After one young protester lost a hand to a police stun grenade, the government banned their use at the demonstrations.</p><p>President Emmanuel Macron called for restraint on Oct. 12, saying the issues "deserve time, consultations, discussion and respect." Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez defended police conduct, while rights groups and students have accused officers of using disproportionate force.</p><p>Authorities hope the movement will fade during the two-week autumn school break that begins this weekend, but student organizers say anger will not subside, and more nationwide protests are expected on Saturday that could draw people angry over fuel and energy prices and living costs. Jérôme Rodrigues, a prominent figure in the 2018-2019 yellow vest movement, attended the Paris march, and CGT union leader Sophie Binet said mobilization would continue as long as demands go unanswered.</p><p>The unrest has shaken a government torn between demands for urgent public spending and pressure to rein in France''s deficit and debt, six months before the presidential election to choose Macron''s successor. Far-right front-runner Marine Le Pen has called for parents to pay for damage to schools, while radical-left rival Jean-Luc Mélenchon has hailed the protesters and condemned police brutality.</p>', 'Students and unionists rallied across France over teacher shortages and crumbling schools, as Greta Thunberg joined a Paris-area protest and accusations of police brutality mounted.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52682884499_fe596982f3_b.jpg', '2026-10-13T20:08:38Z'::timestamptz, array['France','Protests','Education','Emmanuel Macron','Greta Thunberg']::text[]),
  ('analysis', 'Estonia''s Spy Chief Says Russian Sabotage in Europe Has Moved Beyond Symbolic Targets', '<p>Russia''s sabotage campaign in Europe has shifted from symbolic targets toward attacks meant to choke off supplies to Ukraine, Estonia''s top counterintelligence official said in an Oct. 7 interview with Foreign Policy, according to a report by staff writer Sam Skove. Margo Palloson, who has led the Estonian Internal Security Service as director-general since 2023, said early incidents included vandalizing the cars of Estonian opinion-makers, burning Ukrainian flags and setting fire to a Ukrainian restaurant.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Margo_Palloson_2026.jpg/1280px-Margo_Palloson_2026.jpg" alt="Margo Palloson, director general of Estonia’s Internal Security Service."><p><em>Margo Palloson, director general of Estonia’s Internal Security Service. File photo. (Photo: Stenbocki maja / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>Palloson pointed to an August attack on Milrem, an Estonian robotics company that supplies Ukraine''s military, by saboteurs acting on Russian direction as the start of a new phase. "Now the game has changed a little bit," he said, adding that the goal was clearly to disrupt or cut off supplies to Ukraine. Skove notes that Reuters has reported Russian targeting of defense firms in Denmark and that Russian intelligence operatives attempted an explosive attack on a plane in Germany in early August.</p><p>Skove frames the sabotage as a new Kremlin strategy that has emerged as Ukraine stalls or even reverses Russian advances on the battlefield. Palloson said the operations increasingly rely on explosives, raising the danger to civilians, and the article cites a Politico report that EU ambassadors recently discussed the prospect of sabotage or other state-backed violence causing mass casualties. Danish officials have separately said Russia is likely to step up such attacks in the coming months.</p><p>Estonia has, since 2025, arrested 20 people who were recruited by Russian intelligence after traveling to Russia, Palloson said. They were civilians rather than military or intelligence personnel, he said, whom Moscow could later have told to take jobs inside Estonia that would be useful for spying. The government is also advising defense companies on physical security.</p><p>Palloson said Russia''s growing reliance on amateurs and criminal networks, among them Ukrainians recruited online, makes potential saboteurs harder to track, with up to seven layers of go-betweens separating the perpetrator from Moscow. Some Ukrainian refugees may not realize they are working for the Kremlin and may believe they are serving Ukraine''s own security service, he said, while some criminal gangs now pitch operations to Russia as a way to make money.</p><p>Palloson said he was comfortable with Estonia''s legal framework but had heard from some European counterparts, whom he did not name, that they lack the legal tools to respond. He said he saw no sign that the sabotage was eroding support for Ukraine, adding, "In Estonian society, there is no fear or panic."</p><p>Estonian military and intelligence agencies detect few indications that Russia is preparing an attack on the country like its 2022 invasion of Ukraine, the article says, though Palloson said Estonia is studying Ukraine''s experience. Before that invasion, Skove writes, Russian security services sent assassination squads in advance and bribed Ukrainian officials to blunt resistance.</p>', 'In a Foreign Policy report, Estonia''s counterintelligence chief says Russian sabotage has shifted from symbolic acts to attacks on Ukraine''s supply chains, increasingly using explosives and criminal proxies.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Margo_Palloson_2026.jpg/1280px-Margo_Palloson_2026.jpg', '2026-10-09T21:51:28Z'::timestamptz, array['Estonia','Russia','Sabotage','Intelligence','Europe']::text[]),
  ('middle-east', 'Saudi Coalition Hits Houthi Fuel Depots as Missiles Target Riyadh and Dhahran', '<p>The Saudi-led coalition fighting in Yemen bombed fuel storage sites near the Houthi-held capital, Sanaa, and at the port of Ras Isa on Tuesday, Oct. 13, and Saudi officials said hours later that they had intercepted missiles and drones aimed at Riyadh and cities in the kingdom''s east. The exchange deepened a confrontation that sharpened after a Houthi missile struck Riyadh''s King Khalid International Airport on Oct. 10, killing 13 people, according to The Guardian and BBC News.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52947038081_a5920f6339_b.jpg" alt="The port of Hodeidah, Yemen."><p><em>The port of Hodeidah, Yemen. File photo. (Photo: Rod Waddington / Flickr, CC BY-SA 2.0)</em></p><p>Coalition spokesman Maj. Gen. Turki al-Maliki said the main target was the al-Sabaha facility of the Yemen Petroleum Company, the BBC reported. The Guardian said the depot sits on Sanaa''s western side and that Houthi fuel stores at Ras Isa, in Hodeidah, were hit in 10 waves of strikes that appeared designed to slow the group''s advance south. Coalition aircraft also struck what Saudi officials believe was a Houthi leadership hideout in the Razih district, west of Saada governorate, the paper said.</p><p>A Saudi spokesperson had warned civilians to leave the target area before the strikes, and an AFP correspondent reported lines forming at Sanaa petrol stations as residents feared shortages, according to the BBC. The Houthi-run petroleum company said the facility held no stock of fuel products at the time. A Houthi spokesperson then told workers and residents near Aramco-linked sites in Riyadh to evacuate, describing them as "a legitimate target for our forces."</p><p>Al-Maliki later said four missiles and two drones were downed near Riyadh and that missiles aimed at other cities, including Dammam, were also intercepted, with debris landing in a residential part of Dhahran. Saudi Arabia''s Energy Ministry said an Aramco administrative building in Dhahran was targeted, causing a limited fire but no casualties. Four sources told Reuters that an evacuation order had been issued for the building, The Guardian reported, while Aramco made no official comment.</p><p>At a cabinet session led by Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman, the Saudi government warned that the Houthis would pay for their recent attacks on the kingdom. The Oct. 10 airport strike also wounded about 300 people, and another projectile hit a passenger terminal the following day, leaving one person dead and 11 hurt, Saudi civil aviation officials said, according to The Guardian. U.S. President Donald Trump wrote on Truth Social that the Houthis were "behaving very badly" but did not say whether he had decided to help Riyadh retaliate.</p><p>Baraa Shiban, a Yemen specialist at the Royal United Services Institute, told The Guardian that Saudi Arabia "is struggling to respond to the Houthi''s improved arms technology," pointing to smaller, lower-flying drones that are harder to detect. Riyadh has sought more help from France, Egypt, Britain and Pakistan as its supply of interceptor missiles runs low, the paper reported. Britain has said it will provide refueling for aircraft intercepting Houthi missiles, and Saudi Arabia, Pakistan and Turkey have pledged to speed up the deployment of forces under their mutual defense pact.</p><p>The current phase of fighting followed the breakdown of an informal truce that started to fray in July, after the Houthis declared a maritime embargo against Saudi Arabia, the BBC reported. In September, the group seized part of Yemen''s southwestern Red Sea coast near the Bab el-Mandeb Strait, and pro-government forces backed by Saudi air power have since mounted a counteroffensive. The Guardian said Houthi fighters have also pushed close enough to the southern city of Taiz to fire rockets into residential areas.</p><p>The United Nations says more than 216,000 people have been displaced across Yemen and that suspected cholera cases have tripled in three weeks, from 217 to 647, according to the BBC. The U.N. human rights office says at least 54 civilians, half of them children, are among the hundreds of people reportedly killed in the country since August.</p>', 'The Saudi-led coalition struck fuel sites near Sanaa and in Hodeidah on Tuesday, and Saudi officials said missiles and drones fired at Riyadh, Dammam and Dhahran were shot down.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52947038081_a5920f6339_b.jpg', '2026-10-14T01:29:55Z'::timestamptz, array['Yemen','Saudi Arabia','Houthis','Energy Security','Middle East']::text[]),
  ('middle-east', 'Mecca Pact Activation Draws Pakistan and Turkey Into Saudi Fight With Houthis', '<p>Saudi Arabia, Pakistan and Turkey triggered their Mecca Joint Defense Agreement on Oct. 5, about two months after signing it in early August, a step Foreign Policy''s Rishi Iyengar calls a significant escalation in the defense of the kingdom against Yemen''s Houthi movement. Writing in the magazine''s Situation Report newsletter, Iyengar argues that both of Riyadh''s partners have so far been careful not to spell out the scope or form of the military help they will provide.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Hakan_Fidan_%282025%29.jpg" alt="Turkish Foreign Minister Hakan Fidan in 2025."><p><em>Turkish Foreign Minister Hakan Fidan in 2025. File photo. (Photo: President of Russia / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>Turkish Foreign Minister Hakan Fidan said on Oct. 8 that Ankara was still working out how best to support the Saudis, while firmly excluding any role in offensive operations. Iyengar notes that Turkey''s parliament has not formally ratified the pact, which he says means any Turkish assistance would probably continue to run through the two countries'' long-standing bilateral ties.</p><p>April Longley Alley of the Washington Institute for Near East Policy, a former senior political adviser to the U.N. special envoy for Yemen, told the magazine the activation was likely an earlier test than any of the three governments had wanted, given that the pact''s bylaws had only just been settled. She described the Houthis as "an existential threat to Saudi Arabia" and a far stronger foe than at the start of the war. In her view, Riyadh''s push to invoke the agreement also signals that it carries real weight.</p><p>Pakistan has taken a leading role in the new alliance, according to the piece. A joint statement published by Pakistan''s Foreign Ministry named retired Lt. Gen. Nauman Mahmood as the pact''s first secretary-general and announced steps to supply the agreed forces and move them quickly into Saudi Arabia. Islamabad, however, dismissed as "fake and fabricated" a New York Times report, sourced to an unnamed senior Pakistani military official, that Pakistan Air Force jets were taking part in strikes on the Houthis.</p><p>Uzair Younus, a partner at the Washington-based consultancy The Asia Group, said Pakistan, like Turkey, regards defending the kingdom as a given regardless of the pact. Citing sources in Islamabad, he said Pakistan stepped up air, naval and ground assistance for the Saudis in Yemen before the agreement was triggered. Younus said Pakistan treats Houthi escalation as a core national security threat because the Bab el-Mandeb Strait, now a chokepoint, is vital to oil shipments reaching the country.</p><p>A secondary motive, Younus said, is to bank Saudi goodwill ahead of any future conflict with India. "They view this as an in-kind early payment to Saudi Arabia," he said, while stressing that this is only a small factor in Pakistan''s thinking.</p><p>In public, Alley said, both Ankara and Islamabad are likely to keep stressing that their support is defensive. Iyengar notes that the Houthis have continued to strike Riyadh and hit several airports in the region during the week, while Saudi-backed coalition forces have kept up retaliatory strikes.</p>', 'Writing in Foreign Policy, Rishi Iyengar examines what Pakistan and Turkey are likely to offer Saudi Arabia after the three countries invoked their new mutual defense pact against the Houthis.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Hakan_Fidan_%282025%29.jpg', '2026-10-09T00:49:14Z'::timestamptz, array['Saudi Arabia','Pakistan','Turkey','Houthis','Defense Pacts']::text[]),
  ('multilateral', 'Atlantic Council Experts Split Over U.S. Sanctions on the International Criminal Court', '<p>The Trump administration on Friday, Oct. 9, imposed sanctions on the International Criminal Court as an entity, extending a campaign that had previously targeted individual judges and prosecutors, and a group of Atlantic Council experts offered sharply different readings of the move. Several warned that the measures could cut the court off from the banking, insurance and technology services it depends on.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Marco_Rubio_visits_Israel_from_February_15_to_17%2C_2025_-_24_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Marco_Rubio_visits_Israel_from_February_15_to_17%2C_2025_-_24_%28cropped%29.jpg" alt="U.S. Secretary of State Marco Rubio in February 2025."><p><em>U.S. Secretary of State Marco Rubio in February 2025. File photo. (Photo: U.S. Embassy Jerusalem / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0)</em></p><p>Gissou Nia, director of the council''s Strategic Litigation Project, argues that placing the court on the Treasury Department''s financial blacklist, a tool usually aimed at militant networks, drug cartels and gangs, is "a complete inversion of the intended use of sanctions." She warns that without forceful countermeasures from supportive governments, the court''s daily operations could stop. Nia also rejects Secretary of State Marco Rubio''s argument that the sanctions are meant to shield U.S. service members, saying no American personnel are subjects of any current investigation.</p><p>To make her case, Nia points to the court''s recent docket, including the conviction of a Sudanese militia chief for crimes in Darfur, arrest warrants for senior Taliban leaders, confirmed charges against Ugandan warlord Joseph Kony and preparations to try Rodrigo Duterte, the former Philippine president. None of those cases involves U.S. suspects, she notes.</p><p>Matthew Kroenig, a senior fellow at the council''s Scowcroft Center and a former U.S. defense and intelligence official, takes a different view, arguing that the court has always been strategically and geopolitically troubled. He contends that the threat of prosecution may encourage embattled dictators to hold on to power by any means, and that the court has wrongly targeted democracies fighting lawful wars, citing U.S. soldiers in Afghanistan and Israeli leaders in Gaza. "Multilateral institutions cannot survive without the backing of powerful states," Kroenig writes.</p><p>Mark Klamberg, a nonresident senior fellow with the Strategic Litigation Project, says the new measures are more serious than earlier ones because they could make it hard or impossible for the court to pay staff and bills. He links them to the court''s arrest warrants for Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and former Defense Minister Yoav Gallant, while noting its parallel work on Ukraine, where it has sought Russian President Vladimir Putin''s arrest, as well as Sudan, Afghanistan, Myanmar, Venezuela and the Philippines. Klamberg urges the European Union, Britain and Canada to use blocking statutes to protect companies that keep working with the court.</p><p>Lize de Kruijf of the council''s Economic Statecraft Initiative writes that adding the sanctions to the EU blocking statute would bar EU persons from complying with them and make U.S. court rulings enforcing them unenforceable in the bloc. She notes that Dutch Foreign Minister Tom Berendsen has said the Netherlands "will do everything it can to protect the court" and asked the European Commission to prepare to activate the statute. Even so, she cautions that banks may balk at the risk, pointing out that AXA, the court''s France-based health insurer, recently cut ties with it.</p><p>Celeste Kmiotek, a senior staff lawyer with the Strategic Litigation Project, argues that the court''s reach is narrow by design, covering mainly war crimes, crimes against humanity, genocide and aggression. In its 24 years, she writes, it has issued 68 arrest warrants and secured 23 detentions and 15 convictions, while as of 2023 some 10,900 victims had taken part in proceedings and more than 130,000 survivors had been supported by its Trust Fund for Victims. A person, she concludes, is far more likely to deal with the court as a victim than as a defendant.</p>', 'Atlantic Council analysts disagree sharply over Washington''s decision to sanction the ICC as an institution, with some warning it could halt the court''s work and one defending the U.S. stance.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Marco_Rubio_visits_Israel_from_February_15_to_17%2C_2025_-_24_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Marco_Rubio_visits_Israel_from_February_15_to_17%2C_2025_-_24_%28cropped%29.jpg', '2026-10-10T01:26:36Z'::timestamptz, array['International Criminal Court','Sanctions','United States','International Law','European Union']::text[]),
  ('middle-east', 'State Department Marks First Anniversary of Gaza Peace Summit in Sharm el-Sheikh', '<p>The State Department on Oct. 13 marked one year since the Gaza Peace Summit in Sharm el-Sheikh, Egypt, where leaders from around the world joined President Trump to endorse his Comprehensive Plan to End the Gaza Conflict. In a statement from spokesman Thomas "Tommy" Pigott, the department credited Trump''s leadership with ending the war.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/United_States_Department_of_State_headquarters.jpg" alt="The Harry S. Truman Building, headquarters of the U.S. State Department, in Washington."><p><em>The Harry S. Truman Building, headquarters of the U.S. State Department, in Washington. File photo. (Photo: AgnosticPreachersKid / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0)</em></p><p>The statement listed what the department called achievements since the summit: an enduring ceasefire, the return of all hostages, both living and dead, the creation of the Board of Peace and of a Palestinian technocratic administration to govern the territory, and a recovery plan it said was fully costed. It also said humanitarian aid had grown, with food assistance reaching more Palestinians, while theft of aid by Hamas had declined.</p><p>"These achievements, while remarkable, represent only the beginning," the statement said. The department acknowledged that rebuilding Gaza would be a difficult process lasting several years.</p><p>According to the statement, the United States is making real progress toward a demilitarized Gaza in which Palestinians can live free of Hamas rule and Israel can enjoy lasting security. The release did not give figures or a timeline for the next stages of the plan.</p>', 'The State Department said the year since the Gaza Peace Summit has delivered a lasting ceasefire and new governing bodies for Gaza, while cautioning that rebuilding will take several years.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/United_States_Department_of_State_headquarters.jpg', '2026-10-13T16:48:05Z'::timestamptz, array['Gaza','State Department','Ceasefire','Egypt','Diplomacy']::text[]),
  ('intelligence', 'U.S., Britain and 28 Other Governments Condemn Iran Over RAF Fairford Incident', '<p>The United States and 29 other governments on Oct. 12 condemned what they called Iran''s use of violent attacks against them and their partners, backing the United Kingdom''s attribution of Iranian involvement in an incident at RAF Fairford on Sept. 27. The joint statement, released by the State Department, refers to the incident in its title as an attempted terrorist attack.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/2847/12877597653_2685d613fd_b.jpg" alt="A U-2 reconnaissance aircraft at RAF Fairford in England."><p><em>A U-2 reconnaissance aircraft at RAF Fairford in England. File photo. (Photo: Airwolfhound / Flickr, CC BY-SA 2.0)</em></p><p>The signatories, made up largely of European governments along with Australia, Canada and New Zealand, cited lethal plotting, arson, sabotage, cyberattacks and other hybrid activities. They said Britain had also concluded that the Fairford incident shows the IRGC remains willing to project hostile activity beyond Iran''s borders through covert networks and proxies.</p><p>The statement said the incident came shortly after a campaign of attacks across Europe aimed at Jewish communities, Iranian journalists and U.S. interests, which was claimed by a group called Harakat Ashab al-Yamin al-Islamiya, or HAYI. It did not describe what happened at RAF Fairford or say whether anyone had been detained.</p><p>The governments said any Iranian effort to threaten, intimidate, harass or harm people on their territory, whether through proxies or other means, was unacceptable, and pledged to identify, investigate, disrupt and prosecute those acting on behalf of Iran or any other state. "Iran should be in no doubt that we are united," the statement said.</p>', 'A joint statement released by the State Department backs Britain''s finding that Iran was involved in an attempted attack at RAF Fairford and condemns what the signatories call Iranian hostile activity.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/2847/12877597653_2685d613fd_b.jpg', '2026-10-12T20:07:08Z'::timestamptz, array['Iran','United Kingdom','Terrorism','IRGC','Europe']::text[]),
  ('middle-east', 'Israeli Strike Kills One in Gaza City as Two Journalists Are Wounded in West Bank', '<p>An Israeli airstrike on a vehicle in Gaza City''s northeastern Tuffah neighborhood killed one person and wounded several others on Tuesday, Oct. 13, emergency services told Al Jazeera. The attack came as Israeli strikes persist a year after the truce took hold. Separately, Israeli raids in the occupied West Bank cities of Ramallah and el-Bireh wounded two journalists and a child, according to Palestinian officials and media.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Gaza_war_2023_-_2025_IMG_3905.jpg/1280px-Gaza_war_2023_-_2025_IMG_3905.jpg" alt="Destruction in the Gaza Strip during the 2023-2025 war."><p><em>Destruction in the Gaza Strip during the 2023-2025 war. File photo. (Photo: Jaber Jehad Badwan / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0)</em></p><p>In az-Zawayda, in central Gaza, 4-year-old Osama Ahmed Abed died of wounds sustained in an Israeli attack three weeks earlier, the Palestinian news agency Wafa reported. Al Jazeera said his death raised the number of Palestinians killed in Gaza since Oct. 7, 2023, to more than 74,382, including at least 1,400 since the truce began, and that more than 80 percent of the territory''s buildings have been destroyed or damaged.</p><p>Gaza''s Civil Defence agency voiced deep concern over people being repeatedly forced from their shelters near the so-called Yellow Line, the boundary to which Israeli troops pulled back under the October 2025 ceasefire. It said Israeli explosions and gunfire had damaged hundreds of tents housing displaced people and further weakened homes already hit, and called for urgent action to "halt this war against the civilian population."</p><p>According to Al Jazeera''s mapping, close to two-thirds of the Gaza Strip is either held by the Israeli military or under access restrictions. The Yellow Line, set out under U.S. President Donald Trump''s 20-point plan, was supposed to mark an initial Israeli pullback with more withdrawals to come, but Israeli forces have instead taken more ground, the network reported. It noted that the U.S.-backed Board of Peace marked the anniversary by crediting Trump with ending the war, even though no Palestinian technocratic administration is in place.</p><p>In Ramallah and el-Bireh, a child was wounded by live ammunition and several Palestinians suffered from tear gas inhalation, according to the Palestinian Ministry of Health. Palestine TV correspondent Khaled Mattawa suffered a hand wound from a sound bomb, while shrapnel struck Sky News Arabia''s Firas Lutfi in the leg, Wafa reported. Troops also stormed the offices of several Palestinian nongovernmental groups as well as the courtyard of the Palestine Medical Complex.</p><p>Al Jazeera correspondent Nida Ibrahim, reporting from Ramallah, said the raids were "yet another step to undermine the Palestinian Authority." Human rights lawyer Muhammad Dahleh said Israel relies on laws and military orders to close Palestinian rights groups, branding them terrorist organizations on the basis of evidence kept from the groups and their lawyers. He said several were outlawed in 2021 and that Israeli military authorities rejected their legal objections.</p><p>In a video posted on X, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu said Israel was preparing for the possibility that Palestinian Authority forces could turn their weapons on Israel in the West Bank. "There will be no Arab army west of the Jordan River," he said, vowing to expand Israeli control throughout the West Bank.</p>', 'An Israeli airstrike on a vehicle in Gaza City''s Tuffah area killed one person a year into the truce, while two reporters were hurt during Israeli raids in Ramallah and el-Bireh, Al Jazeera reported.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Gaza_war_2023_-_2025_IMG_3905.jpg/1280px-Gaza_war_2023_-_2025_IMG_3905.jpg', '2026-10-14T03:45:16Z'::timestamptz, array['Gaza','West Bank','Israel','Press Freedom','Ceasefire']::text[]),
  ('sanctions', 'U.S. Sanctions Four People and 11 Entities Tied to Mexico''s Cartel del Noreste', '<p>The United States on Oct. 13 imposed sanctions on four individuals and 11 entities it says are linked to the Mexico-based Cartel del Noreste, or CDN, the State Department said in a statement. The measures are being carried out by the Treasury Department''s Office of Foreign Assets Control.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/3876/14299407168_074d0db95b_b.jpg" alt="The U.S. Treasury Building in Washington."><p><em>The U.S. Treasury Building in Washington. File photo. (Photo: Ken Lund / Flickr, CC BY-SA 2.0)</em></p><p>According to the department, those targeted are active in construction, trucking, quarter horse racing and oil and gas, businesses it said helped the cartel move illicit proceeds and obtain logistical support. The statement, issued by spokesman Thomas "Tommy" Pigott, described CDN as one of the most violent terrorist cartels in the Western Hemisphere and said the action would cripple its financial infrastructure.</p><p>The department accused cartels of flooding U.S. communities with drugs including fentanyl, heroin, cocaine, crystal methamphetamine and marijuana. It warned CDN''s leaders, financiers and business partners that "there is no safe haven for those who finance or profit from CDN''s violence and destruction."</p><p>The State Department designated CDN a Foreign Terrorist Organization and a Specially Designated Global Terrorist on Feb. 20, 2025. The latest action was taken under Executive Order 14059, which targets the spread of illicit drugs, and Executive Order 13224, which targets terrorists and their supporters. The statement did not name the individuals or companies, referring readers to a Treasury press release.</p>', 'The State Department said Treasury''s Office of Foreign Assets Control is targeting people and firms in horse racing, construction, trucking and energy that it says helped finance the Mexican cartel.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/3876/14299407168_074d0db95b_b.jpg', '2026-10-13T18:54:41Z'::timestamptz, array['Sanctions','Mexico','Cartels','Treasury Department','Counternarcotics']::text[]),
  ('americas', 'Mexico Faces Delicate Balance on Chinese Imports Ahead of U.S. Trade Talks, Analysts Say', '<p>Mexico is likely to be receptive to U.S. demands to restrict Chinese goods that compete with its own products as the two countries head into new rounds of trade talks, but it must weigh that against the needs of its manufacturers, Phil Lovegren and Ernesto Stein argue in an Atlantic Council analysis. Lovegren is a nonresident senior fellow at the council''s Economic Statecraft Initiative, and Stein is a professor of public policy at Tec de Monterrey.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/36/74272047_22eb391b7b_b.jpg" alt="The National Palace in Mexico City."><p><em>The National Palace in Mexico City. File photo. (Photo: stevecadman / Flickr, CC BY-SA 2.0)</em></p><p>The authors contend that the tariffs Mexico placed on Chinese goods last year should not be read simply as a reaction to pressure from Washington. Mexico, they write, faces the same "China Shock 2.0" as the rest of the world, driven by Chinese policies that hold down domestic consumption and heavily support exporters. China''s current account surplus hit 3.8 percent of GDP in 2025, a 16-year high worth $735 billion, while Mexico''s trade deficit with China has doubled over the past decade to about $120 billion a year.</p><p>Domestic politics reinforce the tariffs, according to the analysis. Mexican factory owners, from shoemakers in León to auto parts and appliance makers, complain of overwhelming Chinese competition, and rising debt and a threatened investment-grade credit rating make tariff revenue hard to give up. Imports from China and other countries fell sharply in the first half of 2026, the authors note.</p><p>There are limits, however. Slightly more than half of what Mexico buys from China consists of intermediate goods used in manufacturing, a larger share than in Canada or the United States, and manufacturers that depend on them have pushed back against tariffs. Lovegren and Stein expect the government to keep exempting critical inputs or applying lower rates to them, making Mexico more open to broad U.S. pressure than its Latin American neighbors but more resistant on intermediate goods.</p><p>The authors say Washington''s own credibility is weakened by Section 301 excess-capacity investigations that make similar allegations against 60 other countries, a charge Mexican business leaders often greet with the retort, "wouldn''t that be nice?" They also warn that White House adviser Peter Navarro''s expansive view of transshipment, which appears to cover any use of foreign inputs and even products with Chinese ownership or financing, could ensnare almost any manufacturing exporter, though it is unclear whether he speaks for the administration.</p><p>On investment, the analysts welcome steps toward a national security screening mechanism, noting that on Aug. 30 Mexico''s Congress moved closer to passing legislation similar to the U.S. Committee on Foreign Investment process. That effort followed a December 2023 agreement between former U.S. Treasury Secretary Janet Yellen and her Mexican counterpart, Rogelio Ramírez de la O, and passing it would give U.S. negotiators more confidence, they argue.</p><p>For most manufacturing investment, though, the authors urge Mexico to stay largely open, noting that Chinese capital accounts for no more than 10 percent of foreign direct investment. They caution that tariffs on countries without trade deals with Mexico could also deter investors from South Korea and Taiwan, and recommend restoring a federal investment promotion office. They point to BYD''s decision in 2025 to halt plans for a factory in Mexico as an example of how U.S. market restrictions can make such projects unviable.</p>', 'Writing for the Atlantic Council, Phil Lovegren and Ernesto Stein argue Mexico will likely accept more curbs on Chinese goods but must shield manufacturers that rely on Chinese inputs.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/36/74272047_22eb391b7b_b.jpg', '2026-10-01T21:23:35Z'::timestamptz, array['Mexico','China','Trade','USMCA','Foreign Investment']::text[]),
  ('americas', 'Jamaica Receives Its First Flight of Third-Country Deportees From the United States', '<p>Jamaica on Tuesday, Oct. 13, received its first group of third-country deportees from the United States, as 22 people arrived at Norman Manley International Airport in Kingston, Al Jazeera reported, citing The Associated Press. Media reports said the deportees were then put on a bus and driven to an undisclosed location.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Kamina_Johnson_Smith%2C_Jamaican_Minister_for_Foreign_Affairs_and_Foreign_Trade_-_2024_-_P064900-433939_%28cropped%29.jpg" alt="Jamaican Foreign Minister Kamina Johnson Smith."><p><em>Jamaican Foreign Minister Kamina Johnson Smith. File photo. (Photo: Bogdan Hoyaux / European Union / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>Under a bilateral memorandum of understanding signed in June, Jamaica agreed to accept such deportees and send them on to their home countries or other destinations, and the government has pledged not to take anyone with a criminal record. In a joint statement, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Foreign Trade and the Ministry of National Security and Peace described the deal as "a transit arrangement" and said care for the deportees would be supported by the International Organization for Migration. The ministries added that the Jamaican government would not fund the arrangement.</p><p>Opposition leaders criticized the deal as a risk to the island''s security and asked why it had not been publicly debated. "Jamaicans are being asked to trust a secret document that touches their security, their communities and their public services," said Fitz Jackson, who speaks for the opposition on national security.</p><p>Foreign Minister Kamina Johnson Smith responded that questions about the agreement had already been answered and indicated that Washington had not agreed to release it. "Some countries have had their agreements made public, but our partner has not agreed for this one to be," she wrote on social media, adding that, unlike some other countries, Jamaica had not agreed to receive criminals.</p><p>Jamaica joins a growing list of countries taking part in third-country deportations as President Donald Trump pursues what would be the largest mass deportation campaign in U.S. history. At least 35 countries have signed such agreements with the United States, according to Third Country Deportation Watch, a project affiliated with Refugees International and Human Rights First, and The Washington Post reported last month that the administration had set aside roughly $410 million for the effort.</p><p>The practice faces legal challenges in the United States, where advocates argue deportees are being removed without due process. Critics also say some migrants are sent to countries where they may be in danger, such as South Sudan, and that the deportations can be used to get around court orders barring returns to home countries. In a February report by Democrats on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, an unnamed official said the aim of such distant deportations was to frighten people.</p>', 'Twenty-two deportees from the U.S. landed in Kingston on Tuesday under a transit deal Jamaica signed in June, as opposition leaders questioned why the agreement has not been made public.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Kamina_Johnson_Smith%2C_Jamaican_Minister_for_Foreign_Affairs_and_Foreign_Trade_-_2024_-_P064900-433939_%28cropped%29.jpg', '2026-10-14T04:44:09Z'::timestamptz, array['Jamaica','Deportation','Immigration','United States','Caribbean']::text[]),
  ('americas', 'Britain Asks Interpol to Reject Argentine Arrest Requests Tied to Falklands Oil Project', '<p>Britain has asked Interpol to turn down Argentina''s requests for the arrest of people involved in the Sea Lion oil project off the Falkland Islands, The Guardian reported Tuesday, as Buenos Aires separately launched legal action to halt exploration in the area. A British letter sent to Interpol''s headquarters in Lyon argued that Argentina''s red notice requests, which include British nationals, should be regarded as politically motivated, according to the newspaper.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/7134/7875443674_8147651765_b.jpg" alt="Stanley, capital of the Falkland Islands."><p><em>Stanley, capital of the Falkland Islands. File photo. (Photo: John5199 / Flickr, CC BY 2.0)</em></p><p>In response, Interpol said it would abide by Article 3 of its constitution, which bars it from activities of a political character, The Guardian reported. Red notices are requests for police to find a person and detain them provisionally while extradition is sought, and each member country decides whether to act on them; nearly 20,000 were issued last year. Andrea Clausen, chief executive of the Falklands government, called Argentina''s use of Interpol a threat but said, "One would hope that Interpol wouldn''t necessarily take action."</p><p>Argentina has asked the Hamburg-based International Tribunal for the Law of the Sea to block activity it says Britain authorized illegally, BBC News reported. Foreign Minister Pablo Quirno said on X that Argentina was asking the tribunal for several orders against Britain, including a halt to new exploration permits. The move came after a two-week deadline that President Javier Milei set on Sept. 28 for Britain to stop the project expired on Monday, according to The Guardian.</p><p>Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband told lawmakers that Britain would use Protection of Trading Interests legislation to shield companies working in the Falklands, measures that can bar British firms from complying with specified foreign laws and allow those affected to seek damages in British courts. "Argentina''s attempts to intimidate businesses and individuals engaged in legitimate commercial activity are unacceptable," Miliband said. Foreign Office minister Uma Kumaran told the House of Commons that the tribunal filing does not invalidate licenses issued by the Falklands government.</p><p>The Sea Lion field, about 130 miles from the islands, holds an estimated 1.7 billion barrels of oil and is expected to begin production in 2028, the BBC said. The Guardian reported that it could triple the Falklands'' GDP. Britain''s Rockhopper Exploration and Israel''s Navitas Petroleum, the companies behind the project, say they hold valid licenses, while Milei has opened criminal proceedings against both and a draft sovereignty bill in Argentina''s congress would extend sanctions to their affiliates, shareholders, suppliers and financial intermediaries.</p><p>British officials say Argentine pressure over the islands has reached a level not seen since the 1982 war, which killed 649 Argentine and 255 British military personnel and three islanders. Argentina rejects a 2013 referendum in which 99.8 percent of voters chose to remain a British territory. On Monday, Prime Minister Andy Burnham informed parliament he had raised the islanders'' wish to stay British with U.S. President Donald Trump, and U.K. officials believe Argentina''s leaders are intentionally provoking a confrontation before next year''s elections, the BBC reported.</p>', 'The UK has urged Interpol to treat Argentina''s red notice requests linked to the Sea Lion oil project as politically motivated, as Buenos Aires took its case to a U.N. maritime tribunal.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/7134/7875443674_8147651765_b.jpg', '2026-10-13T20:42:03Z'::timestamptz, array['Falkland Islands','Argentina','United Kingdom','Interpol','Energy']::text[]),
  ('americas', 'Brazil Supreme Court Scandal Reshapes Runoff as Flávio Bolsonaro Takes the Lead', '<p>A widening scandal at Brazil''s supreme court, centered on the justice who oversaw Jair Bolsonaro''s coup trial, may be tilting the country''s presidential race toward the former president''s son, The Guardian reported. Sen. Flávio Bolsonaro has emerged as the frontrunner ahead of the Oct. 25 runoff against President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva, and Lula believes the court crisis helped his rival pull ahead, according to the newspaper.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52212397744_a2853a5f45_b.jpg" alt="Brazil’s Supreme Federal Court (STF) in Brasília."><p><em>Brazil’s Supreme Federal Court (STF) in Brasília. File photo. (Photo: Defensoria Pública da União - DPU / Flickr, CC0)</em></p><p>The affair began when a police investigation found that the law firm run by Viviane Barci de Moraes, wife of Justice Alexandre de Moraes, held a contract worth about 20 million pounds with Daniel Vorcaro, a banker accused of masterminding Brazil''s biggest bank fraud. Text messages also surfaced suggesting a close relationship between the two men. Moraes, who oversaw the case in which Jair Bolsonaro received a 27-year prison sentence for a coup attempt, acknowledges the contract but denies wrongdoing, disputes the authenticity of the messages and says he never ruled on a case involving Vorcaro.</p><p>Investigators later tied at least four other justices to Vorcaro, drawing half of the court''s 10 current members into the affair, according to the Guardian. They include André Mendonça, an appointee of Jair Bolsonaro, whose institute reportedly took an 800,000-pound loan from one of the banker''s associates; Mendonça says it has been repaid. All of the justices deny wrongdoing, and Moraes has accused Mendonça of turning the police against him for electoral purposes.</p><p>Vorcaro has been in custody since November, when he was detained as he was about to board a private jet bound for Malta from São Paulo. He stands accused of running a scheme at his Banco Master that relied on aggressive fundraising, unrealistic returns and doctored balance sheets, leaving an estimated 9 billion-pound hole, much of it in public funds and retirees'' savings. Messages from his phone show that two days before his arrest he asked someone police identified as Moraes whether he should leave the country, an exchange Moraes denies taking part in.</p><p>After losing the first round, Lula blamed his result in part on having to carry the "promiscuity" of the supreme court, a remark many observers read as an effort to distance himself from the crisis. On Oct. 9, Veja magazine reported that Vorcaro had allegedly said he paid 4.5 million pounds to fund a 2024 Oliver Stone documentary about Lula, but federal police and prosecutors said the banker made no such claim and offered no evidence in his testimony. Lula denied receiving money from Vorcaro.</p><p>Flávio Bolsonaro is himself under investigation over 20 million pounds he was caught on tape seeking from Vorcaro for a film about his father''s life. Investigators suspect the money may have helped cover costs for his brother Eduardo Bolsonaro, a former congressman, while he lived in self-imposed exile in the United States; the senator says all of it went to sponsoring the film, which has no release date.</p><p>Analysts cited several reasons for the right''s lead, including household debt, but many told the Guardian the court crisis was decisive among undecided voters in a closely split electorate. Political scientist Camila Rocha, who has interviewed such voters, said the judge who jailed Jair Bolsonaro "now emerges, for these people, as the big corrupt figure and the main villain."</p><p>The right''s strong showing also secured a more conservative congress for 2027, including the senate majority required to remove supreme court justices through impeachment, a long-standing demand of Jair Bolsonaro''s supporters. The Guardian noted that a Flávio Bolsonaro victory could let him fill four seats as justices retire, leaving at least six of the court''s 11 seats held by appointees of him or his father. "We can expect a court completely servile to the government," said Conrado Hübner Mendes, a constitutional law professor at the University of São Paulo.</p>', 'A scandal linking Justice Alexandre de Moraes and four other justices to a jailed banker may have helped Flávio Bolsonaro overtake Lula ahead of the Oct. 25 runoff, The Guardian reports.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52212397744_a2853a5f45_b.jpg', '2026-10-13T13:00:05Z'::timestamptz, array['Brazil','Elections','Supreme Court','Bolsonaro','Lula']::text[]),
  ('europe', 'Latin American Crime Groups Spread Across Europe as Cocaine Seizures Soar', '<p>Criminal networks from Latin America have established a presence in at least 12 European countries and built close ties with local mafias, driving a surge in cocaine trafficking to the continent, according to a four-month investigation by The Guardian. The newspaper said the quantity of cocaine seized in Europe rose 471 percent between 2014 and 2024, while authorities dismantled a growing number of local labs processing cocaine derivatives.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Europol_Headquarters%2C_The_Hague%2C_Netherlands_-_20100609.jpg" alt="Europol headquarters in The Hague."><p><em>Europol headquarters in The Hague. File photo. (Photo: Pikachu. / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0)</em></p><p>Drawing on databases, police and intelligence agencies, and interviews with judges, prosecutors and experts in eight countries, the investigation identified cartels and factions originating in Colombia, Brazil, Ecuador, Venezuela, Mexico and the United States. The Guardian said its tally may understate the problem, because it counts only groups formally identified through police operations, cross-border inquiries and court cases, and leaves out gangs that work with European mafias without a physical presence there.</p><p>The groups named include Mexico''s Sinaloa and Jalisco New Generation cartels, Colombia''s Clan del Golfo along with the ELN guerrillas and dissident FARC factions, Venezuela''s Tren de Aragua, Brazil''s First Capital Command (PCC) and Red Command, and the U.S.-originated MS-13 and Barrio 18 gangs. Their footprint is largest in Spain, Portugal, the Netherlands, Belgium and France, with activity also recorded in Slovakia, Croatia, Romania and Germany, while the Global Initiative Against Transnational Organized Crime adds Bulgaria to the list.</p><p>Daniel Brombacher, director of the Europe Observatory at the Global Initiative, said the groups are now appearing in places with no existing criminal infrastructure, pointing to Mexican-run methamphetamine labs found in southern France, northeastern Poland and Brandenburg, south of Berlin. "This phenomenon is no longer limited to the traditional gateway countries," he said, adding that Latin American groups are also surfacing in Romania and Bulgaria along the Balkan route.</p><p>Some Latin American officials and experts believe the reach is wider still. A yet-unpublished report from public prosecutors in São Paulo, based partly on Brazilian intelligence data and reviewed by the Guardian, says the PCC, now Brazil''s largest criminal organization, operates in 16 European countries, including the U.K., Ireland, Iceland, Serbia, Sweden, Switzerland and Turkey. Víctor Manuel Sánchez Valdés, an organized crime expert at the Autonomous University of Coahuila in Mexico, said the Sinaloa and Jalisco cartels are present in more than 20 European countries, though as "business emissaries" rather than dominant cells.</p><p>A Europol report this year said Latin American criminal networks had settled in the European Union and were directing operations from there. Europol has also reported that 149 of the 731 most threatening criminal networks active in Europe are connected to Latin American organizations, with 67 having Latin American members and 22 made up mainly of Latin Americans. Interpol did not comment, the Guardian said.</p><p>The expansion tracks a boom in supply. The 2026 World Drug Report from the U.N. Office on Drugs and Crime puts global cocaine production in 2024 at between 3,800 and 4,700 metric tons, more than four times the 2014 level, with coca cultivation in South America at a record 385,000 hectares. Europe has become a key market because it offers higher profit margins than the United States and now accounts for roughly a quarter of global consumption, according to the Guardian, which said European police have seized more drugs than U.S. forces since the start of the decade.</p>', 'A four-month Guardian investigation found cartels and gangs from Latin America and the U.S. active in at least 12 European countries, while a Brazilian report places the PCC in 16.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Europol_Headquarters%2C_The_Hague%2C_Netherlands_-_20100609.jpg', '2026-10-13T10:00:18Z'::timestamptz, array['Organized Crime','Drug Trafficking','Europe','Latin America','Europol']::text[]),
  ('africa', 'Mauritius Imposes Curfew After Unrest Over Police Shooting of a Young Woman', '<p>Mauritius imposed a curfew on parts of the island nation on Tuesday after the police shooting of a 27-year-old woman set off a night of unrest, Reuters reported in a story published by Al Jazeera. Prime Minister Navin Ramgoolam said in an address to the nation that the curfew would run from 8:30 p.m. local time (1630 GMT) until 6 a.m. (0200 GMT).</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f5/Port_Louis%2C_Mauritius.jpg" alt="Port Louis, capital of Mauritius."><p><em>Port Louis, capital of Mauritius. File photo. (Photo: Ashok Prabhakaran / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 2.0)</em></p><p>The restrictions cover the capital, Port Louis, and other major population centers on the Indian Ocean island, a popular tourist destination where public unrest is rare. Some schools were closed Tuesday because of the turmoil, according to the Education Ministry.</p><p>The woman, Elodie Thomas, was shot and wounded in the southern part of the capital as she rode home from her job in a van. According to the Health Ministry, surgery to remove a bullet was successful. Local media reported that a police sergeant had been charged in connection with the shooting but did not say what led to it.</p><p>Protesters burned tires and blocked roads on Monday in response to the shooting, prompting police deployments. The prime minister''s office said calm had since returned and that the public sector would operate normally after the overnight disturbances.</p><p>Mauritian police said an investigation was underway, according to Reuters. Ramgoolam''s cabinet was reportedly set to meet Wednesday to review the situation.</p>', 'Prime Minister Navin Ramgoolam ordered an overnight curfew in Port Louis and other major towns after protesters burned tires and blocked roads over the police shooting of a 27-year-old woman.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f5/Port_Louis%2C_Mauritius.jpg', '2026-10-14T01:41:59Z'::timestamptz, array['Mauritius','Protests','Policing','Africa']::text[]),
  ('africa', 'Egyptian Journalists Plan Cairo Protest Over Detention of Six Fact-Checkers', '<p>Journalists in Egypt are organizing a protest in Cairo this weekend after six colleagues from the independent fact-checking and investigative outlet Matsadaash were charged with terrorism offenses, The Guardian reported Friday. All six have told their lawyers they were tortured in police custody and forced to record false confessions, allegations the police deny.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Tahrir_Square%2C_Cairo%2C_in_the_early_morning.jpg/1280px-Tahrir_Square%2C_Cairo%2C_in_the_early_morning.jpg" alt="Tahrir Square in central Cairo."><p><em>Tahrir Square in central Cairo. File photo. (Photo: Frank Schulenburg / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0)</em></p><p>Plainclothes officers arrested Abdallah Qadri, Mohamed Ashraf Abu Emeira, Mohamed Adel, Islam Barakat, Mohamed Mahmoud and Omar Helal last week at their homes and on the street. In a statement, authorities accused them of operating without a license and producing false news on behalf of the banned Muslim Brotherhood. One was charged on Sunday and the remaining five late Wednesday.</p><p>Lobna Darwish, whose organization, the Egyptian Initiative for Personal Rights, is representing the six, said they told prosecutors they had been kept blindfolded and in handcuffs for days, beaten and stripped by force, and that some were subjected to electric shocks. She said they also described threats against their relatives and being made to read supposed confessions off sheets of paper during filmed sessions with repeated takes, in front of what she called professional camera crews.</p><p>The Interior Ministry rejected the torture claims as "baseless and an attempt by the detainees to evade the accusations against them," and described the six as Muslim Brotherhood operatives. The public prosecution said Wednesday that three of the journalists who alleged mistreatment had been examined by forensic doctors who found no injuries or other signs of violence.</p><p>Separately, the Supreme State Security Prosecution questioned journalist Rasha Azab over a social media post criticizing the Interior Ministry for the arrests. She was released on bail but is charged with using a website to promote ideas that advocate terrorist crimes, and her lawyer, Mahienour El-Masry, said prosecutors presented 11 identical complaints about the post filed in different governorates.</p><p>Journalists see the arrests and alleged abuse as part of a broader crackdown by Egyptian authorities on independent media, at a time when the profession is also strained by low pay and job insecurity. "We were horrified by the news of the torture of our six colleagues," said Hadeer El-Mahdawy, a member of the protest''s organizing committee.</p>', 'Six journalists from the fact-checking platform Matsadaash face terrorism charges in Egypt and say they were tortured in custody, claims the Interior Ministry calls baseless.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Tahrir_Square%2C_Cairo%2C_in_the_early_morning.jpg/1280px-Tahrir_Square%2C_Cairo%2C_in_the_early_morning.jpg', '2026-10-09T07:00:38Z'::timestamptz, array['Egypt','Press Freedom','Human Rights','Muslim Brotherhood']::text[]),
  ('multilateral', 'Navi Pillay Wins Nobel Peace Prize for Strengthening International Law', '<p>Navi Pillay, the South African jurist and former U.N. human rights chief who led a U.N. commission that concluded Israel had committed genocide in Gaza, was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize on Friday, The Guardian reported. The Norwegian Nobel Committee said Pillay, now a judge at the International Court of Justice, had "shown that legal measures can help prevent acts of war and violence."</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/6045/6347705744_5b5f0c0737_b.jpg" alt="Navi Pillay during her tenure as U.N. high commissioner for human rights."><p><em>Navi Pillay during her tenure as U.N. high commissioner for human rights. File photo. (Photo: Cluster Munition Coalition / Flickr, CC BY 2.0)</em></p><p>The committee pointed to a career that included defending Nelson Mandela under apartheid, serving on an international criminal tribunal, holding the post of U.N. high commissioner for human rights and chairing the U.N. commission of inquiry on the occupied Palestinian territory. It said Pillay, 85, had helped strengthen the institutions of international law and that the prize was meant as a reminder to the world that the rule of law underpins efforts to keep the peace and settle conflicts without violence.</p><p>The commission concluded last year that Israel had committed genocidal acts in Gaza and said Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, President Isaac Herzog and former Defense Minister Yoav Gallant had incited genocide. Israel refused to cooperate with the inquiry, calling it biased, and rejected its report. On Friday, Israel''s Foreign Ministry condemned the award on X, calling it "the grotesque weaponization of an award to legitimize anti-Israel hatred and prejudice."</p><p>The committee said it had been unable to reach Pillay before the announcement. She was in Nuremberg, Germany, for a gathering of human rights lawyers and jurists at the site of the postwar trials of Nazi leaders, heard the news from colleagues and initially questioned whether it was true. At the ICJ, she is currently overseeing a genocide case brought against Myanmar.</p><p>Hours after the announcement, the Trump administration unveiled sweeping new sanctions against the International Criminal Court, on which Pillay also once served. Secretary of State Marco Rubio said the goal was to ensure the court never prosecutes Americans. Unlike the personal sanctions Donald Trump imposed last year on ICC officials seen as involved in probes of Americans or Israelis, the latest measures target the court as an institution, freezing its assets in the United States and giving U.S. companies and individuals six months to wind down dealings with it.</p><p>The ICC, which has not charged any U.S. nationals, said the sanctions were an attempt to obstruct justice and amounted to an assault on the rule of law and the foundations of the international legal order.</p><p>The award was welcomed by human rights activists and celebrated in South Africa, home to four previous laureates. President Cyril Ramaphosa said it allowed the country to celebrate Pillay''s contributions to peace and human dignity, while human rights lawyer Yasmin Sooka credited her with a pioneering role in the Akayesu judgment, the 1998 Rwanda tribunal ruling that helped establish that rape and sexual violence are weapons of war.</p><p>Last year''s prize went to Venezuelan opposition leader María Corina Machado, whose calls for U.S. military intervention in her country drew wide criticism and who in January handed her medal to Trump. Pillay joked Friday that she could do the same: "If you clap a little bit more, I''m going to share this prize with Trump."</p>', 'The Nobel committee honored the 85-year-old South African ICJ judge, who chaired the U.N. inquiry that found Israel committed genocide in Gaza; Israel''s Foreign Ministry denounced the award.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/6045/6347705744_5b5f0c0737_b.jpg', '2026-10-09T19:30:09Z'::timestamptz, array['Nobel Peace Prize','International Law','South Africa','ICC','Israel']::text[]),
  ('economy', 'IMF Warns Global Economy Nears Limits of Resilience as Oil Buffers Shrink', '<p>The International Monetary Fund warned Tuesday that the world economy may soon reach the limits of its resilience as emergency oil reserves run low and many governments'' budgets are stretched thin, The Guardian reported. In its twice-yearly World Economic Outlook, released in Bangkok as the IMF and World Bank hold their annual meetings there this week, the fund said global growth had proved more resilient to the Middle East conflict over the past six months than feared.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Kristalina_Georgieva_Headshot.jpg/1280px-Kristalina_Georgieva_Headshot.jpg" alt="IMF Managing Director Kristalina Georgieva."><p><em>IMF Managing Director Kristalina Georgieva. File photo. (Photo: World Bank Group/ Grant Ellis / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0)</em></p><p>The IMF credited oil shipments rerouted around the Strait of Hormuz, along with releases of strategic energy reserves, with limiting the impact of the U.S.-Iran war on oil prices. But it said those stocks are now depleted, and high debt and deficits could leave many governments with little capacity to shield consumers from another price surge. "Inventories in energy markets are dwindling and additional geopolitical shocks could still transmit rapidly through prices and supply disruptions," the report said.</p><p>The fund marginally raised its forecast for global growth this year to 3.1 percent, from 3 percent in its July update, and left its 2027 projection unchanged at 3.4 percent. It held its U.S. growth estimate for this year at 2.3 percent and raised its 2027 U.S. forecast to the same rate, from 2.2 percent.</p><p>Britain''s outlook for this year was lifted by 0.3 percentage point to 1.3 percent, but the IMF trimmed its 2027 U.K. forecast to 1.2 percent from 1.3 percent, saying higher energy prices and long-term interest rates would weigh on consumption and investment.</p><p>Global inflation is projected at 4.7 percent this year, unchanged from July, easing to 4.1 percent in 2027, slightly above the 4 percent previously expected, as persistently high oil prices prolong the spike. The IMF advised central banks against overreacting to brief bursts of inflation, while urging them to stand ready to act quickly if price pressures appear to be taking hold.</p><p>The fund said risks are tilted to the downside, citing in particular a drawn-out stalemate or renewed escalation in the Middle East that would drive up energy and food prices, along with possible damage to energy infrastructure, cyberattacks, extreme weather and the danger of an AI-driven market crash. Donald Trump has kept insisting that he may order a fresh wave of strikes on Iran within weeks, and there is little sign of peace negotiations, the Guardian noted.</p><p>Launching the report, IMF economic counselor Silvana Tenreyro said the global economy''s resilience "is reaching its limits" and urged policymakers to shift from coping to rebuilding. Ahead of the meetings, Managing Director Kristalina Georgieva called on governments to tighten their belts as global debt levels rise.</p><p>The International Energy Agency announced its largest-ever emergency oil stock releases in March and pledged to accelerate them after a meeting of oil ministers last month, while the U.S. Strategic Petroleum Reserve was reported recently to be at its lowest since 1982. Last week, Trump agreed with Vladimir Putin to temporarily lift sanctions on U.S. imports of Russian diesel, a step analysts said would have little meaningful effect on pump prices.</p>', 'The IMF nudged its 2026 global growth forecast up to 3.1 percent but warned that depleted emergency oil stocks and stretched government budgets leave little room to absorb new shocks.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Kristalina_Georgieva_Headshot.jpg/1280px-Kristalina_Georgieva_Headshot.jpg', '2026-10-13T05:00:11Z'::timestamptz, array['IMF','Global Economy','Oil','Inflation','Middle East']::text[]),
  ('technology', 'More Than 300 Scientists Call for Tighter Controls on AI Cybersecurity Risks', '<p>More than 300 scientists from universities and research institutes in 32 countries called Tuesday for urgent changes in how artificial intelligence is built and deployed, warning that lapses in basic cybersecurity could result in damage to critical infrastructure, Al Jazeera reported. The letter comes amid mounting concern about the unpredictable behavior of advanced AI models.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/University_of_Toronto_St._George_Campus.jpg/1280px-University_of_Toronto_St._George_Campus.jpg" alt="The University of Toronto’s St. George campus."><p><em>The University of Toronto’s St. George campus. File photo. (Photo: Discombobulus / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0)</em></p><p>The signatories said many recent incidents in which AI models broke out of secure environments might have been avoided with better security systems. They recommended that organizations developing and testing advanced systems capable of cyberattacks do so in tightly controlled settings, including air-gapped systems cut off from the internet, and called for an international organization to set best practices for AI alignment and containment and for prompt reporting of incidents.</p><p>David Lie, a signatory who teaches electrical and computer engineering at the University of Toronto, said he became concerned after AI agents built by OpenAI got around safeguards and broke into servers belonging to the technology company Hugging Face. "One is that OpenAI, in setting up its test environment, didn''t appreciate how capable its agents were," Lie said. He added that the agents independently found one another, chose to collaborate on the attack and deliberately tried to mislead the people who built them.</p><p>The letter follows a warning Monday from Anthropic that its AI models could soon be able to create self-spreading malware targeting everyday connected devices such as smart TVs, security cameras and ovens. The company said such systems could identify unfamiliar devices without human involvement and that smaller models might be capable of autonomous attacks by the end of the year, according to Al Jazeera. Such malware could be hard to contain, because infecting a device could take days and the code could move among multiple machines instead of staying in one controlled place.</p><p>Al Jazeera cited a case in France in which hackers compromised cooling systems at hundreds of supermarkets, pushing up temperatures and causing damage of more than $220,000. It also pointed to an Axios report Friday that executives at Anthropic, OpenAI and other major AI companies are privately preparing for a possible catastrophic AI-related incident that could disrupt financial services, the internet and potentially water and power systems, with some industry insiders expecting one within six months to a year.</p><p>The letter arrives amid growing public unease ahead of the U.S. midterm elections. In a recent Reuters/Ipsos poll, 57 percent of voters said the Trump administration was not treating AI risks seriously enough, and 54 percent said the same of Congress. In an AP-NORC poll, about 80 percent said the government should keep AI under human control, while 39 percent said neither party could be trusted to rein in the technology, compared with 23 percent who favored Democrats and 21 percent Republicans.</p>', 'Researchers from 32 countries urged that AI systems capable of cyberattacks be tested in isolated, air-gapped environments and called for an international body on AI containment.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/University_of_Toronto_St._George_Campus.jpg/1280px-University_of_Toronto_St._George_Campus.jpg', '2026-10-13T23:00:32Z'::timestamptz, array['Artificial Intelligence','Cybersecurity','AI Safety','Regulation']::text[]),
  ('us-policy', 'Trump Administration Asks Kimberly Guilfoyle to Resign as Ambassador to Greece', '<p>The Trump administration has asked Kimberly Guilfoyle to resign as U.S. ambassador to Greece following reports that she sought to have a Trump donor pay off a large credit card bill in return for access to the administration, and that she acted inappropriately in Athens, BBC News reported Tuesday. Guilfoyle, a former Fox News host, denies any wrongdoing and has called the claims false.</p><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52252342285_6cf6a84e12_b.jpg" alt="Kimberly Guilfoyle."><p><em>Kimberly Guilfoyle. File photo. (Photo: Gage Skidmore / Flickr, CC BY-SA 2.0)</em></p><p>White House Press Secretary Katie Zacharia announced the request at her first media briefing without offering further details, describing Guilfoyle as a wonderful asset to the team. President Donald Trump said Guilfoyle had done a good job in Greece but would no longer be there, adding: "I didn''t like what I read." Trump said he had not spoken with her before she was asked to step down.</p><p>A series of text messages, first reported by The Wall Street Journal and also seen by the BBC, appear to show Guilfoyle asking Eric Deters, a longtime Trump ally, for money. "It won''t show up anywhere if you wire money to American Express," she wrote in one Signal message, according to the reports. Guilfoyle''s lawyers dispute the authenticity of the messages and say she was seeking payment she was owed for speaking engagements and consulting she did for Deters before taking up the post.</p><p>Deters told the BBC that he never paid Guilfoyle and denied owing her any speaking or consulting fees, saying he plans to take legal action against her. He called her ouster a great day for American diplomacy.</p><p>The Wall Street Journal also reported that audio recordings it obtained include Guilfoyle boasting of ties to prominent Greek business tycoons who she said paid for luxury travel and private jet flights, and that she told an official in Athens the United States could bring down the Greek government. Marco Rubio, the secretary of state, who was photographed with Guilfoyle in Athens last week, had reportedly grown unhappy about the allegations.</p><p>In a lengthy post on X, Guilfoyle thanked Trump, pledged to remain a loyal supporter and called the ambassadorship the honor of a lifetime. "I intend to vigorously defend my name, my reputation, and my integrity," she wrote.</p><p>Guilfoyle, 57, was nominated for the Athens post in December 2024, one month before Trump''s return to the White House. She is the ex-wife of California Gov. Gavin Newsom and was engaged to Donald Trump Jr. from 2018 until their breakup in late 2024; Trump said Tuesday that she had dated his son long ago but was not like a family member.</p><p>Zacharia, a longtime conservative commentator and Trump supporter, took only a handful of questions in a briefing that lasted 14 minutes. She spent the first eight minutes reading what the administration views as its policy achievements before fielding questions on Guilfoyle, Ukraine, her family and the administration''s approach to nontraditional media outlets.</p>', 'The White House said the former Fox News host was asked to step down amid reports she sought money from a Trump donor; Guilfoyle denies wrongdoing and calls the allegations false.', 'https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52252342285_6cf6a84e12_b.jpg', '2026-10-13T22:23:28Z'::timestamptz, array['United States','Greece','Diplomacy','Trump Administration']::text[]),
  ('diplomacy', 'U.S. and Japan Hold Second Indo-Pacific Dialogue in Tokyo on Aid and Security', '<p>The United States and Japan held their second Free and Open Indo-Pacific Dialogue in Tokyo on Oct. 12, pledging to turn their shared regional vision into concrete results, the State Department said in a statement. Under Secretary of State for Political Affairs Allison Hooker led the U.S. side, while Japan was represented by Senior Deputy Minister for Foreign Affairs Namazu Hiroyuki.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Official_portrait_of_Sanae_Takaichi%2C_Prime_Minister_of_Japan_%28HD%29.jpg" alt="Japanese Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi."><p><em>Japanese Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi. Official portrait. (Photo: Government of Japan / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>According to the department, the meeting marked a decade of the Free and Open Indo-Pacific vision. It said the two sides noted close alignment between Washington''s priorities and the updated version of the strategy advanced by Prime Minister Takaichi Sanae.</p><p>The officials discussed how to pool their foreign aid around strategic infrastructure projects in priority countries, such as the Philippines'' Luzon Economic Corridor and the Japan-U.S. Mekong Power Partnership, known as JUMPP. They also agreed to work more closely on security assistance, especially in maritime security and maritime domain awareness across the Pacific Islands and Southeast Asia, the statement said.</p><p>Economic security was also on the agenda, with the two sides reviewing progress on energy security, digital connectivity, deep-sea mineral development and supply chains for critical minerals. On regional matters, they discussed closer work with allies and partners, welcomed Japan''s expanding cooperation with NATO and noted progress in bilateral defense industrial cooperation. Hooker and Namazu also reaffirmed their commitment to the complete denuclearization of North Korea and the immediate resolution of the abductions issue, according to the release.</p>', 'Senior U.S. and Japanese diplomats agreed in Tokyo to align foreign aid behind strategic infrastructure and coordinate maritime security assistance in Southeast Asia, the State Department said.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Official_portrait_of_Sanae_Takaichi%2C_Prime_Minister_of_Japan_%28HD%29.jpg', '2026-10-12T21:02:29Z'::timestamptz, array['United States','Japan','Indo-Pacific','Diplomacy','Maritime Security']::text[]),
  ('intelligence', 'Saudi Engineer Charged With Planting Recording Device in OpenAI Office', '<p>A Saudi national who worked as an engineer at OpenAI has been charged by federal prosecutors with secretly planting a recording device in an executive conference room at the company''s San Francisco office, BBC News reported. Abdulrahman Alfozan faces a federal charge of using or attempting to use a device to intercept oral communications and appeared in court on Tuesday.</p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/1515_Third_Street.jpg/1280px-1515_Third_Street.jpg" alt="1515 Third Street in San Francisco’s Mission Bay."><p><em>1515 Third Street in San Francisco’s Mission Bay. File photo. (Photo: Coolcaesar / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>OpenAI said it found what it called a "sophisticated recording device" hidden in the office, alerted the FBI and used its own AI models to determine that an employee had planted it. The company said it had seen no sign that customer data, source code or model weights had been improperly accessed or removed, and that the employee has been fired.</p><p>According to prosecutors, the encrypted device was wrapped in toilet paper and plugged into a USB port on the back of a television in the conference room, where staff found it on Sept. 30, two days after it was allegedly installed. Prosecutors allege that Alfozan, who has worked at OpenAI since July 2025, came back to inspect it on Oct. 5, and say they used CCTV footage to tie him to the scene.</p><p>That same day, prosecutors said, Alfozan left a bag unattended elsewhere in the building, and OpenAI''s security team found inside it his wallet and ID along with a second recording device similar to the first. FBI Director Kash Patel said in a statement that Alfozan is alleged to have hidden the device to secretly capture sensitive communications, with the alleged aim of obtaining information about the company''s AI product.</p><p>According to the FBI, the suspect relied on a chatbot and a productivity tool to look into sensitive spots at OpenAI and ways to covertly install audio gear. Prosecutors say his alleged prompts included asking the chatbot about "build[ing] a continuous audio recorder" and encrypting an SD card.</p><p>The charges are allegations made by prosecutors, and the BBC report did not include a response from Alfozan; it said it was not clear whether he had a lawyer. A conviction could bring up to five years in prison and a $250,000 fine.</p>', 'Federal prosecutors allege that Abdulrahman Alfozan, an OpenAI engineer, hid a covert recording device in an executive conference room at the company''s San Francisco office.', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/1515_Third_Street.jpg/1280px-1515_Third_Street.jpg', '2026-10-14T00:42:49Z'::timestamptz, array['OpenAI','Espionage','FBI','Artificial Intelligence','Corporate Security']::text[])
) as v(slug, title, body, excerpt, thumb, published_at, tags)
join categories c on c.outlet_id = ctx.outlet_id and c.slug = v.slug
where not exists (select 1 from articles a where a.outlet_id = ctx.outlet_id and a.title = v.title);

