• Photo: Cluster Munition Coalition / Flickr (CC BY 2.0) — https://www.flickr.com/photos/25900134@N03/6347705744 (keep the credit line)
• Rewritten from published reporting with attribution. Verify facts against the original before major edits.'),
      ('IMF Warns Global Economy Nears Limits of Resilience as Oil Buffers Shrink', '• Source names the UK chancellor as ''John Healey'' (expected in Bangkok later this week); name omitted from copy - verify before adding.
• Source once calls the report the ''World Economic Report''; the official title World Economic Outlook was used, as elsewhere in the source.
• Figures are from the Guardian''s summary of the WEO; check against the IMF tables if possible.
• Related-link line about an AI share crash and financial stability was treated as navigation noise apart from the one mention in the body.
• Source (The Guardian): https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/oct/13/imf-global-economy-world-economic-outlook-uk-gdp-forecast-2026
• Photo: World Bank Group/ Grant Ellis / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=78460483 (keep the credit line)
• Rewritten from published reporting with attribution. Verify facts against the original before major edits.'),
      ('More Than 300 Scientists Call for Tighter Controls on AI Cybersecurity Risks', '• Al Jazeera story by Andy Hirschfeld; the letter''s title, organizers and publishing venue are not named in the source.
• Anthropic warning, Axios report and OpenAI/Hugging Face incident are secondhand via Al Jazeera (the last only via David Lie''s account); verify independently.
• Recommended-story links (fired OpenAI staff, Anthropic police tip, etc.) were treated as navigation noise.
• Poll dates and sample details not given in source.
• Source (Al Jazeera): https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/10/13/scientists-urge-tighter-ai-controls-to-prevent-global-cyber-risks?traffic_source=rss
• Photo: Discombobulus / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=150105559 (keep the credit line)
• Rewritten from published reporting with attribution. Verify facts against the original before major edits.'),
      ('Trump Administration Asks Kimberly Guilfoyle to Resign as Ambassador to Greece', '• All misconduct claims are allegations from WSJ/BBC-reviewed messages and WSJ audio; Guilfoyle denies them and her lawyers dispute the messages'' authenticity - keep attribution intact in any edit.
• Deters'' account (never paid, owed nothing) contradicts Guilfoyle''s lawyers; he is a party to the dispute.
• Source says one message was dated days before her Senate confirmation hearing ''last July''; year is ambiguous, so the date was omitted.
• Source does not say whether Guilfoyle has formally resigned; BBC related-link lines at the end were ignored.
